{"id":27,"role":"Assistant","content":"Let me read the rest of the axiom context and look at the key infrastructure. Let me also search for the Seiler lecture notes in parallel.\nTime: 2026-08-06 04:13:32 UTC","timestamp":1785989612,"token_count":42,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"limit\":108,\"offset\":2590,\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean\"}","name":"read_file"},"id":"1c3d3cb8-7a4d-4c5f-aa9f-119da37f5859","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\":\"Seiler Gauge Theories Constructive Quantum Field Theory Statistical Mechanics 1982 lattice reflection positivity transfer matrix\",\"max_results\":8,\"source\":\"web\"}","name":"search_literature"},"id":"0409cfad-28ed-4ce3-96ef-de99fe6a5b77","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":3403,"reasoning_content":"Let me understand the structure better. The axiom `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` is what we need to close. It states:\n\n```\n0 ≤ ∫ cfg, osG N T L β f (U_ext cfg) * osG N T L β f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) ∂ μ₀\n```\n\nwhere `osG N T L β f U = f U * exp(-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β U) * exp(-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U / 2)`.\n\nSo the integral is `∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0` where `G(U) = f(U) · exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2)`.\n\nThe key insight from session 35 is that the single-step transfer matrix kernel IS PD. But the obstacle is the σ twist.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The recommended path forward is:\n1. Read Seiler (1982) lecture notes\n2. Study BFS \"half gauge fields\" approach\n3. Formalize the single-step transfer matrix kernel PD property\n4. Reduce reflection positivity to single-step positivity\n5. Consider operator approach T = B*B\n\nLet me first try to find the Seiler (1982) lecture notes online, since that's the most accessible source for the actual proof mechanism. This is important because the σ twist handling is the key obstacle, and I need to understand how the literature handles it.\n\nLet me search for the Seiler lecture notes and the BFS half gauge fields approach. These are the key references that would tell me how the σ twist is handled.\n\nActually, let me think about this strategically. The breakthrough is that the single-step kernel IS PD. But the reflection positivity has the σ twist. The key question is: can reflection positivity be REDUCED to single-step positivity?\n\nLet me think about the math here. The reflection positivity is:\n∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nwhere G(U) = f(U) · exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2).\n\nThe factorization in the code shows:\nexp(-β S_total) = [exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2)] · [exp(-β S⁺(θU)) · exp(-β S⁰(θU)/2)]\n              = G_pos(U) · G_pos(θU)  (since S⁻(U) = S⁺(θU) and S⁰(θU) = S⁰(U))\n\nSo the integral is ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · G_pos(U) · G_pos(θU) dμ₀.\n\nNow, the key insight from the design doc: this is ⟨θg, T^{2n}g⟩ which reduces to ⟨θf, f⟩ where f = Tg.\n\nWait, but actually the issue is more subtle. Let me think about the structure.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The most promising approach according to the design doc is (3) + (4): formalize the single-step kernel PD property, then reduce reflection positivity to it.\n\nBut there's a fundamental issue identified in session 34 (§8.11.38): character expansion gives ∑ A² (NOT ∑ |A|²), which is a fundamental obstacle. And session 35 found that the single-step kernel IS PD (avoiding this obstacle by using gauge-fixing).\n\nLet me think about whether the reflection positivity can be reduced to the single-step positivity.\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is:\nI = ∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀(U)\n\nwhere G(U) = f(U) · exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2).\n\nNow, the full Boltzmann factor is exp(-β S_total) = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁻) · exp(-β S⁰).\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on spatial links. The key relationship is:\n∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀ = ⟨g, T^{2n} g⟩_θ  (reflection inner product)\n\nHmm, but actually this is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about what's the most productive thing to do.\n\nThe design doc says the MOST PROMISING approach is (3) + (4). But there's a deep obstacle: the σ twist. Session 35 found that the σ twist is an ARTIFACT of the reduction to ∫ F·F(σ) dμ, and the original path integral has INDEPENDENT bra and ket.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The key realization is:\n\nThe reflection positivity ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0 is equivalent to showing that the transfer matrix T is a POSITIVE OPERATOR with respect to the REFLECTION inner product ⟨f, g⟩_θ = ∫ f(U) · g(θU) dμ.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach is the operator approach T = B*B. Let me think about this.\n\nThe transfer matrix T = B*B where B is the \"half-step\" operator. Then ⟨f, Tf⟩ = ‖Bf‖² ≥ 0. But the reflection positivity is ⟨θg, T^{2n}g⟩, not ⟨f, Tf⟩.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider the structure. Actually, I realize I should look at the literature to understand the actual mechanism. The Seiler (1982) lecture notes are the most accessible source. Let me search for them.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach. The key issue is:\n\n1. The single-step kernel K_temporal(u_s, v_s) = ∑_γ (|c_γ|²/d_γ) χ_γ(W(0)·W(1)) IS PD. This is for the transfer matrix that integrates out ONE temporal slice.\n\n2. The reflection positivity requires ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0, which involves the σ twist.\n\nThe question is: how does the single-step PD property imply reflection positivity?\n\nLet me think about the structure of the path integral. For T time steps (T odd, so T = 2n+1), the path integral is:\nZ = ∫ ∏_t ∏_x B_p(U_p(t,x)) dμ(U)\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on spatial links at a fixed time. The path integral factorizes as:\nZ = Tr(T^{T-1})  (roughly, with interface terms)\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The OS decomposition splits the action into S⁺ (positive times), S⁻ (negative time), S⁰ (interface). With T = 2n+1, the positive half has n+1 time slices (t = 0, ..., n) and the negative half has n time slices (t = n+1, ..., 2n), with the interface at t = 0 (or t = n, depending on convention).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me focus on what's actionable.\n\nI think the most productive thing to do is:\n1. Search for the Seiler (1982) lecture notes and BFS half gauge fields to understand the actual mechanism.\n2. In parallel, look at the existing code infrastructure to understand what's available.\n\nLet me search the literature first. Let me also look at the existing infrastructure in the code.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The design doc §8.11.39 says the σ twist is an ARTIFACT. The key insight is that the original path integral has INDEPENDENT bra and ket. So maybe the approach is:\n\nInstead of reducing to ∫ F·F(σ) dμ (which introduces the σ twist), we should keep the bra and ket INDEPENDENT and show that the resulting kernel is PD.\n\nLet me think about this. The reflection positivity is:\n∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nwhere G(U) = f(U) · exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2).\n\nNow, θU is the reflection of U. The key is that S⁺(θU) = S⁻(U) and S⁰(θU) = S⁰(U).\n\nSo G(U) · G(θU) = f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2) · exp(-β S⁻(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2)\n                = f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total(U))\n\nThis is the full Boltzmann factor. So the integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total(U)) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nNow, the key insight: exp(-β S_total) is the FULL Boltzmann factor, which is PD on the full link group (proven by boltzmannFactorPD). But the integral ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·K(U) dμ is NOT the standard PD quadratic form ∫ f(U)·conj(f(U))·K(U) dμ. It's ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·K(U) dμ, which involves the reflection θ.\n\nSo the question is: is ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·K(U) dμ ≥ 0 when K is PD and f is supported on the positive spatial interface?\n\nThis is the \"reflection positivity\" property, which is STRONGER than just PD of K.\n\nHmm. So the PD of the Boltzmann factor alone is NOT sufficient. We need the reflection positivity property.\n\nOK here's the key. Let me think about what reflection positivity actually requires.\n\nReflection positivity: ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S(U)) dμ ≥ 0 for all f supported on the positive half.\n\nThis is NOT the same as PD of exp(-β S). PD says ∫ f(U) · conj(f(U)) · exp(-β S(U)) dμ ≥ 0. Reflection positivity says ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S(U)) dμ ≥ 0.\n\nThe difference: conj(f(U)) vs f(θU). For REAL-valued f, conj(f(U)) = f(U), so PD says ∫ f(U)² · exp(-β S) dμ ≥ 0 (trivially true since everything is non-negative... wait, no, exp(-β S) > 0 and f² ≥ 0, so yes trivially ≥ 0). But reflection positivity says ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S) dμ ≥ 0, which is NOT trivial because f(U)·f(θU) can be negative.\n\nSo the reflection positivity is a genuinely stronger condition. The key is the STRUCTURE of exp(-β S) — specifically, how it factorizes across the reflection plane.\n\nNow, the factorization is:\nexp(-β S_total) = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰) · exp(-β S⁻)\n              = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2) · exp(-β S⁰/2) · exp(-β S⁻)\n              = G_pos(U) · G_pos(θU)\n\nwhere G_pos(U) = exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2) and G_pos(θU) = exp(-β S⁻(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2) (using S⁺(θU) = S⁻(U), S⁰(θU) = S⁰(U)).\n\nSo the integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · G_pos(U) · G_pos(θU) dμ₀(U)\n\nNow, let g(U) = f(U) · G_pos(U). Then the integral is:\n∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nThis is the \"reflection inner product\" ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ₀(U).\n\nSo reflection positivity is: ⟨g, g⟩_θ ≥ 0 for all g of the form f · G_pos where f is supported on the positive spatial interface.\n\nNow, the key question: WHY is ⟨g, g⟩_θ ≥ 0?\n\nThe answer from the literature (Osterwalder-Seiler, BFS): the Boltzmann factor has a specific PRODUCT STRUCTURE (product of plaquette factors), and each plaquette factor is a PD function. The reflection positivity follows from this product structure.\n\nBut session 34 found that the character expansion gives ∑ A² (not ∑ |A|²), which is an obstacle. And session 35 found that the single-step kernel IS PD via gauge-fixing.\n\nLet me think about the gauge-fixing approach for reflection positivity.\n\nThe key insight from session 35: the σ twist is an ARTIFACT of the reduction to ∫ F·F(σ) dμ. The original path integral has INDEPENDENT bra and ket.\n\nSo maybe the approach is: DON'T reduce to ∫ F·F(σ) dμ. Instead, keep the bra and ket independent and show the kernel is PD.\n\nBut the axiom is literally ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0, which IS the reduced form with the σ twist. So we need to prove THIS.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read the axiom. The axiom is:\n```\n0 ≤ ∫ cfg, osG N T L β f (U_ext cfg) * osG N T L β f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) ∂ μ₀\n```\n\nSo it's ∫ G(U) · G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0 where G = osG = f · exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2).\n\nAnd we showed this equals ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ₀.\n\nSo we need to prove ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ₀ ≥ 0.\n\nNow, the approach from session 35: the single-step kernel IS PD. Can we use this?\n\nLet me think about the transfer matrix approach. The path integral with T = 2n+1 time steps can be written as:\nZ = ∫ ... = Tr(T^n · V · T^n)  or something like that\n\nwhere T is the transfer matrix and V is the interface operator.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The reflection positivity ⟨g, g⟩_θ ≥ 0 where g = f · G_pos.\n\nThe key: G_pos(U) = exp(-β S⁺(U)) · exp(-β S⁰(U)/2). This is the Boltzmann factor for the POSITIVE half (including half the interface).\n\nNow, S⁺ involves plaquettes in the positive time region (t > 0). S⁰ involves plaquettes at the interface (t = 0, involving temporal links at t = 0).\n\nThe transfer matrix T integrates out one temporal slice. So G_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2) can be written as a product of transfer matrices.\n\nSpecifically, if the positive half has n+1 time slices (t = 0, 1, ..., n), then:\nexp(-β S⁺) = ∏_{t=0}^{n-1} (transfer matrix from t to t+1)\n\nAnd exp(-β S⁰/2) is half the interface contribution.\n\nSo g = f · ∏ T · (interface/2).\n\nAnd ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ₀.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about the simplest case: T = 1 (single time step, so n = 0, the positive half is just t = 0).\n\nWait, T must be odd and NeZero, so T ≥ 1. T = 1 means there's only one time slice (t = 0). Then S⁺ = 0 (no plaquettes in positive time), S⁻ = 0, S⁰ = interface action. But with T = 1, there are no temporal plaquettes at all (the temporal direction has only one slice, so temporal links wrap around). Hmm, this is a degenerate case.\n\nLet me think about T = 3 (n = 1). Then the positive half has t = 0, 1 and the negative half has t = 2. The interface is at t = 0.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me focus on the literature search to understand the actual mechanism, and in parallel look at the code.\n\nLet me search for the Seiler lecture notes and BFS approach. Let me also look at the existing code infrastructure.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what's the most productive use of this session.\n\nThe key breakthrough is documented. The next steps are:\n1. Read Seiler (1982) — understand the mechanism\n2. Study BFS half gauge fields\n3. Formalize single-step kernel PD\n4. Reduce reflection positivity to single-step positivity\n5. Operator approach T = B*B\n\nI think the most productive thing is to:\n1. Search for the literature to understand the mechanism (especially how the σ twist is handled)\n2. Look at the existing code to understand what infrastructure is available\n3. Based on that, decide on a concrete formalization plan\n\nLet me start with the literature search and code exploration in parallel.\n\nLet me search for Seiler 1982 and BFS half gauge fields. And let me look at the existing infrastructure: plaquetteBoltzmannPD, the character expansion, the transfer matrix construction.\n\nLet me be strategic about context usage. I have 202000 tokens. Let me use them wisely.\n\nLet me first do the literature searches and look at key code sections.","turn":13,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":28,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 2697 lines, 157635 bytes --\n2590 | form `∫∫ f(g) conj(f(h)) K(g⁻¹ h) dμ dμ ≥ 0` (which follows from PD-ness of `K`).\n2591 | Instead, it is a *single* integral `∫ f(g) f(θg) K(g) dμ` with the geometric\n2592 | reflection `θ` and `K` evaluated at `g` (not `g⁻¹ h`).  PD-ness of `K` on the\n2593 | group does NOT imply this integral is non-negative; the Peter–Weyl character\n2594 | expansion of `K` and character orthogonality are needed to decompose the\n2595 | integrand into `|Fourier coefficients|²`.  See `docs/gap_analysis.md` for the\n2596 | full analysis.\n2597 | -/\n2598 | \n2599 | /-- **Clean factorization of the reflection-positivity integrand.**\n2600 | \n2601 | `osG(U) · osG(θU) = f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_W(U))`.\n2602 | \n2603 | This is a purely algebraic identity: it follows from the reflection symmetries\n2604 | `S⁺(θU) = S⁻(U)` and `S_int(θU) = S_int(U)`, together with the action\n2605 | decomposition `S_W = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int`.  No support hypothesis on `f` is needed. -/\n2606 | lemma osG_thetaG_factorization\n2607 |     (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T)\n2608 |     (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ)\n2609 |     (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) :\n2610 |     osG N T L β f U * osG N T L β f (reflectLinkVariable N U) =\n2611 |     f U * f (reflectLinkVariable N U) *\n2612 |     Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) U) := by\n2613 |   unfold osG\n2614 |   have h_pos_reflect : wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β (reflectLinkVariable N U) =\n2615 |       wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β U :=\n2616 |     (neg_action_reflection_os_periodic N T L β hT U).symm\n2617 |   have h_int_reflect : wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (reflectLinkVariable N U) =\n2618 |       wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U :=\n2619 |     interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic N T L β hT U\n2620 |   have h_total : wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) U =\n2621 |       wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β U + wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β U +\n2622 |       wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U :=\n2623 |     total_decomposition_os_periodic N T L β U\n2624 |   rw [h_pos_reflect, h_int_reflect, h_total]\n2625 |   -- Split the RHS exp(-β*(S⁺+S⁻+S_int)) into a product of four exp's matching the LHS\n2626 |   rw [show (-β * (wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β U + wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β U +\n2627 |       wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U) : ℝ) =\n2628 |       (-β * wilsonActionOSPositive N T L β U) + (-β * wilsonActionOSNegative N T L β U) +\n2629 |       (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U) by ring]\n2630 |   rw [Real.exp_add, Real.exp_add]\n2631 |   rw [show (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U : ℝ) =\n2632 |       (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U / 2) + (-β * wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β U / 2)\n2633 |       by ring]\n2634 |   rw [Real.exp_add]\n2635 |   ring\n2636 | \n2637 | structure PeriodicExpectation (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) : Type 1 where\n2638 |   /-- The partition function Z = ∫ exp(-S_W) dU. -/\n2639 |   partitionFunction : ℝ\n2640 |   /-- Positivity of the partition function: Z > 0 for β > 0. -/\n2641 |   partitionFunctionPos : partitionFunction > 0\n2642 |   /-- The expectation of an observable F. -/\n2643 |   evaluate : (LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) → ℝ\n2644 |   /-- Linearity of expectation. -/\n2645 |   linearity : ∀ (a b : ℝ) (F G : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n2646 |     (MeasureTheory.Integrable (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n2647 |       F (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\n2648 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\n2649 |       (productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)))) →\n2650 |     (MeasureTheory.Integrable (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n2651 |       G (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\n2652 |       Real.exp (-β * wilsonActionFiniteConfig N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\n2653 |       (productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)))) →\n2654 |     evaluate (λ U => a * F U + b * G U) = a * evaluate F + b * evaluate G\n2655 |   /-- Normalization: ⟨1⟩ = 1. -/\n2656 |   normalization : evaluate (λ _ => 1) = 1\n2657 |   /-- Positivity: if F ≥ 0 pointwise, then evaluate F ≥ 0. -/\n2658 |   positivity : ∀ (F : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n2659 |     (∀ U, F U ≥ 0) → evaluate F ≥ 0\n2660 |   /-- Reflection positivity: if f depends only on positive + spatial-interface links, then ⟨f · θ(f)⟩ ≥ 0. -/\n2661 |   reflectionPositive : ∀ (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n2662 |     dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface N T L f →\n2663 |     evaluate (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) ≥ 0\n2664 | /--\n2665 | Construct a `PeriodicExpectation` for the Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice.\n2666 | This uses the Osterwalder-Seiler factorization to prove reflection positivity without\n2667 | the `hadd` limitation of the Z4Site version.\n2668 | -/\n2669 | noncomputable def wilsonPeriodicExpectation (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) :\n2670 |     PeriodicExpectation N T L β hT :=\n2671 |   {\n2672 |     partitionFunction := partitionFunctionFinite N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n2673 |     partitionFunctionPos := partitionFunctionFinite_pos N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n2674 |     evaluate := gibbsExpectation N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n2675 |     linearity := λ a b F G hF hG =>\n2676 |       gibbsExpectation_linear N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) a b F G hF hG\n2677 |     normalization := gibbsExpectation_normalization N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n2678 |     positivity := λ F hF => gibbsExpectation_pos N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) F hF\n2679 |     reflectionPositive := λ f hf_supported =>\n2680 |       gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive N T L β hT f hf_supported\n2681 |   }\n2682 | /--\n2683 | The SU(N) lattice Yang-Mills measure with the Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice\n2684 | is reflection positive for observables that depend only on links in the positive-time\n2685 | and **spatial** interface (time-0) regions.  Temporal interface links (μ=0 at t=0)\n2686 | are excluded: they are integrated out as part of the transfer matrix kernel\n2687 | (Lüscher decomposition), avoiding the σ-twist obstacle.  This is the content of\n2688 | the Osterwalder-Seiler theorem.\n2689 | -/\n2690 | theorem lattice_ym_reflection_positive_periodic (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hβ : β > 0) (hT : Odd T) :\n2691 |     ∀ (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ),\n2692 |       dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface N T L f →\n2693 |       (wilsonPeriodicExpectation N T L β hT).evaluate (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) ≥ 0 := by\n2694 |   intro f hf_supported\n2695 |   exact (wilsonPeriodicExpectation N T L β hT).reflectionPositive f hf_supported\n2696 | \n2697 | end PeriodicSite\n\nTime: 2026-08-06 04:13:33 UTC","timestamp":1785989613,"token_count":2333,"tool_call_id":"1c3d3cb8-7a4d-4c5f-aa9f-119da37f5859","full_token_estimate":1541,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":2697,"byte_count":157635,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":13,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":29,"role":"Tool","content":"---\nTitle: Matrix Product States for Lattice Field Theories\nAuthors: Mari Carmen Bañuls; Krzysztof Cichy; J. Ignacio Cirac; Karl Jansen; Hana Saito\nAbstract: The term Tensor Network States (TNS) refers to a number of families of states that represent different ansätze for the efficient description of the state of a quantum many-body system. Matrix Product States (MPS) are one particular case of TNS, and have become the most precise tool for the numerical...\n\n---\nTitle: Applications of Jarzynski's relation in lattice gauge theories\nAuthors: Alessandro Nada; Michele Caselle; Gianluca Costagliola; Marco Panero; Arianna Toniato\nAbstract: Jarzynski's equality is a well-known result in statistical mechanics, relating free-energy differences between equilibrium ensembles with fluctuations in the work performed during non-equilibrium transformations from one ensemble to the other. In this work, an extension of this relation to lattice g...\n\n---\nTitle: Recent progress in lattice supersymmetry: from lattice gauge theory to black holes\nAuthors: Daisuke Kadoh\nAbstract: Supersymmetry (SUSY) is a fascinating topic in theoretical physics, because of its unique and counterintuitive properties. It is expected to emerge as new physics beyond the standard model, and it is also a building block for supergravity and superstring theory. A number of exact results obtained vi...\n\n---\nTitle: Mean Field Approaches to Lattice Gauge Theories: A Review\nAuthors: Pierpaolo Fontana; Andrea Trombettoni\nAbstract: Due to their broad applicability, gauge theories (GTs) play a crucial role in various areas of physics, from high-energy physics to condensed matter. Their formulations on lattices, lattice gauge theories (LGTs), can be studied, among many other methods, with tools coming from statistical mechanics ...\n\n---\nTitle: Theories with global gauge anomalies on the lattice\nAuthors: P. Mitra\nAbstract: A global anomaly in a chiral gauge theory manifests itself in different ways in the continuum and on the lattice. In the continuum case, functional integration of the fermion determinant over the whole space of gauge fields yields zero. In the case of the lattice, it is not even possible to define a...\n\n---\nTitle: Heavy-Quark Masses from the Fermilab Method in Three-Flavor Lattice QCD\nAuthors: Elizabeth D. Freeland; Andreas S. Kronfeld; James N. Simone; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We report on heavy quark mass calculations using Fermilab heavy quarks. Lattice calculations of heavy-strange meson masses are combined with one-loop (automated) lattice perturbation theory to arrive at the quark mass. Mesons are constructed from Fermilab heavy quarks and staggered light quarks. We ...\n\n---\nTitle: Scalar lattice gauge theory\nAuthors: C. Wetterich\nAbstract: Scalar lattice gauge theories are models for scalar fields with local gauge symmetries. No fundamental gauge fields, or link variables in a lattice regularization, are introduced. The latter rather emerge as collective excitations composed from scalars. For suitable parameters scalar lattice gauge t...\n\n---\nTitle: Lattice formulations of supersymmetric gauge theories with matter fields\nAuthors: Anosh Joseph\nAbstract: Certain classes of supersymmetric gauge theories, including the well known N=4 supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory, that takes part in the AdS/CFT correspondence, can be formulated on a Euclidean spacetime lattice using the techniques of exact lattice supersymmetry. Great ideas such as topological fiel...\n\nTime: 2026-08-06 04:13:33 UTC","timestamp":1785989613,"token_count":886,"tool_call_id":"0409cfad-28ed-4ce3-96ef-de99fe6a5b77","full_token_estimate":740,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"search_literature","file_path":null,"old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1666,"edit_line":null},"turn":13,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":30,"role":"Assistant","content":"The literature search didn't surface the key references directly. Let me search more specifically and examine the existing code infrastructure in parallel.\nTime: 2026-08-06 04:19:17 UTC","timestamp":1785989957,"token_count":42,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"num_results\":8,\"query\":\"Osterwalder Seiler 1978 reflection positivity lattice gauge field theory transfer matrix proof mechanism\"}","name":"web_search"},"id":"c73d6927-0500-40c9-9639-122d88bf5265","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\":\"def transferMatrix|transferMatrixPositivity|integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg|transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect\",\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"ab6a98e6-0413-43e2-986c-82074277269f","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":4707,"reasoning_content":"The literature search didn't find the Seiler lecture notes directly. Let me try a more targeted search. Let me also look at the existing code infrastructure to understand what's available.\n\nLet me search more specifically for the Seiler 1982 lecture notes and the BFS half gauge fields approach. Let me also look at the key infrastructure in the code.\n\nLet me think about what I know and what I need to find out.\n\nThe key question is: how is reflection positivity proven for lattice gauge theory? The mechanism is:\n1. The Boltzmann factor factorizes as a product of plaquette factors.\n2. Each plaquette factor is a PD function (proven by plaquetteBoltzmannPD).\n3. The reflection positivity follows from this product structure.\n\nBut the obstacle (session 34) is that the character expansion gives ∑ A² (not ∑ |A|²).\n\nThe breakthrough (session 35) is that the single-step kernel IS PD via gauge-fixing.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a way to use the gauge-fixing approach for reflection positivity directly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the problem. The reflection positivity integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total(U)) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nwhere f depends only on positive spatial interface links (dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface).\n\nThe key: f doesn't depend on temporal interface links (μ=0 at t=0). So f only depends on:\n- Links at positive times (t > 0): both spatial and temporal\n- Spatial links at t = 0 (μ ≠ 0)\n\nThe σ twist (reflection θ) inverts temporal links and reflects spatial links. For the interface spatial links, θ fixes them (θ(u⁰_s) = u⁰_s). For temporal links, θ inverts them.\n\nSince f doesn't depend on temporal interface links, f(θU) = f(U) on the interface spatial links (which are fixed by θ). But f(θU) ≠ f(U) in general because θ reflects the positive-time links.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. The reflection θ maps:\n- t → -t (mod T)\n- temporal links at t → inverted temporal links at -t\n- spatial links at t → spatial links at -t\n\nSo for a link at positive time t > 0, θ maps it to a link at negative time -t. And f depends on positive-time links, so f(θU) depends on negative-time links (the reflection of positive-time links).\n\nSo f(U) · f(θU) couples positive-time and negative-time links.\n\nNow, the Boltzmann factor exp(-β S_total) = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁻) · exp(-β S⁰).\n\nS⁺ depends on positive-time plaquettes (couples positive-time links).\nS⁻ depends on negative-time plaquettes.\nS⁰ depends on interface plaquettes (couples interface links).\n\nThe integral is:\n∫ f(U⁺) · f(θU⁺) · exp(-β S⁺(U⁺)) · exp(-β S⁻(U⁻)) · exp(-β S⁰(U⁰)) dμ\n\nwhere U⁺ = positive-time links, U⁻ = negative-time links, U⁰ = interface links.\n\nNow, S⁻(U⁻) = S⁺(θU⁻) = S⁺(U⁺ reflected). And f(θU⁺) depends on U⁻ (the reflection of U⁺).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about the transfer matrix approach.\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on spatial links at a fixed time. The kernel is:\nK(u_s, v_s) = ∫ ∏_x B_p(u_t(x) · v_s(x) · u_t(x+1)⁻¹ · u_s(x)⁻¹) dμ(u_t)\n\nThis integrates out the temporal links between two time slices, giving a kernel that maps spatial links at time t to spatial links at time t+1.\n\nThe path integral with T time steps is:\nZ = ∫ ... = Tr(T^{T-1} · V)  or similar\n\nwhere V is the interface operator.\n\nFor reflection positivity, we need:\n∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ ≥ 0\n\nwhere g = f · G_pos = f · exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2).\n\nNow, G_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2) is the Boltzmann factor for the positive half. This can be written as a product of transfer matrices:\nG_pos = T^n · V^{1/2}  (roughly)\n\nwhere T is the transfer matrix (n applications for n time steps) and V^{1/2} is half the interface.\n\nSo g = f · T^n · V^{1/2}.\n\nAnd ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ = ⟨f · T^n · V^{1/2}, f · T^n · V^{1/2}⟩_θ.\n\nHmm, this is the reflection inner product. The key property is that T commutes with θ (the reflection). So:\n⟨f · T^n · V^{1/2}, f · T^n · V^{1/2}⟩_θ = ⟨θ(f · T^n · V^{1/2}), f · T^n · V^{1/2}⟩\n= ⟨θf · T^n · V^{1/2}, f · T^n · V^{1/2}⟩  (using T commutes with θ, V^{1/2} commutes with θ)\n\nWait, this isn't quite right. Let me be more careful.\n\nThe reflection inner product is ⟨a, b⟩_θ = ∫ a(U) · b(θU) dμ = ⟨θa, b⟩ where θa(U) = a(θU).\n\nSo ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ⟨θg, g⟩ = ∫ g(θU) · g(U) dμ.\n\nNow, g = f · G_pos. And θg = θf · θG_pos = θf · G_neg (where G_neg = θG_pos = exp(-β S⁻) · exp(-β S⁰/2)).\n\nSo ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ θf(U) · G_neg(U) · f(U) · G_pos(U) dμ\n            = ∫ θf(U) · f(U) · exp(-β S_total(U)) dμ.\n\nThis is the same as before. So the reflection inner product ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ.\n\nNow, the key: can we write this as ⟨h, Th⟩ for some h and positive T?\n\nIf T = B*B (positive), then ⟨h, Th⟩ = ‖Bh‖² ≥ 0.\n\nThe transfer matrix T is the operator that integrates out one temporal slice. The full Boltzmann factor is a product of transfer matrices.\n\nLet me think about this. The full path integral is:\nZ = ∫ exp(-β S_total) dμ = Tr(T^{T-1})  (for periodic BC, roughly)\n\nAnd the reflection positivity integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ = ⟨f, T^{T-1} f⟩_θ  (reflection inner product)\n\nHmm, but this involves the reflection inner product, not the standard inner product.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is different. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe reflection positivity is about the REFLECTION INNER PRODUCT ⟨a, b⟩_θ = ∫ a(U) b(θU) dμ. We need ⟨g, g⟩_θ ≥ 0 for g = f · G_pos.\n\nThe claim is that the transfer matrix T is POSITIVE with respect to the reflection inner product. I.e., ⟨g, Tg⟩_θ ≥ 0 for all g.\n\nIf T = B*B where B commutes with θ, then:\n⟨g, Tg⟩_θ = ⟨g, B*B g⟩_θ = ⟨Bg, Bg⟩_θ = ‖Bg‖²_θ ≥ 0  (if the reflection inner product is positive semi-definite)\n\nBut wait, the reflection inner product ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ g(U) g(θU) dμ is NOT positive semi-definite in general! That's the whole point — we need to PROVE it's ≥ 0 for specific g.\n\nHmm, so this approach is circular. The reflection inner product is NOT a genuine inner product (it's not positive semi-definite in general). We need to prove it's ≥ 0 for g = f · G_pos.\n\nOK so let me think about this differently. The key is the PRODUCT STRUCTURE of G_pos.\n\nG_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2) = ∏_{plaquettes in positive half} B_p · ∏_{interface plaquettes} B_p^{1/2}\n\nEach B_p is a PD function. The product of PD functions is... not necessarily PD. But the product structure across the reflection plane is what gives reflection positivity.\n\nActually, I think the key mechanism is the Lüscher decomposition / transfer matrix factorization. Let me think about the simplest non-trivial case.\n\nActually, let me look at the literature more carefully. Let me search for the actual proof mechanism. The key references are Osterwalder-Seiler (1978) and BFS (1979). Let me search for these specifically.\n\nLet me also look at the existing code to understand the transfer matrix construction and what's already proven.\n\nLet me search for the Osterwalder-Seiler paper and the BFS paper, and look at the transfer matrix code.\n\nActually, I realize I should focus on understanding the actual mathematical mechanism. Let me think about it from first principles.\n\nThe reflection positivity for lattice gauge theory:\n\nThe key insight (from Lüscher, Osterwalder-Seiler): the Boltzmann factor can be written as a product of \"transfer matrix\" kernels, each of which is a PD function. The reflection positivity then follows from the positivity of the transfer matrix.\n\nSpecifically, the transfer matrix T has kernel:\nK(u_s, v_s) = ∫ ∏_x B_p(plaquette(t, t+1, x)) dμ(u_t)\n\nThis kernel is PD (proven in session 35 via gauge-fixing + character expansion).\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ = ⟨f, T^{2n} f⟩_θ\n\nwhere T^{2n} is the transfer matrix applied 2n times (for T = 2n+1 time slices).\n\nNow, the key: T is self-adjoint and POSITIVE (as an operator on L²). And T commutes with θ.\n\n⟨f, T^{2n} f⟩_θ = ⟨θf, T^{2n} f⟩ = ⟨T^n θf, T^n f⟩ = ⟨θ T^n f, T^n f⟩ = ⟨θg, g⟩\n\nwhere g = T^n f. So the reflection positivity reduces to ⟨θg, g⟩ ≥ 0 for g = T^n f.\n\nBut ⟨θg, g⟩ = ∫ g(θU) g(U) dμ is the reflection inner product, which is NOT automatically ≥ 0.\n\nHmm, so this doesn't immediately work. Unless... the reflection inner product IS ≥ 0 for g in the range of T^n?\n\nWait, I think I'm confusing myself. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the reflection inner product ⟨θg, g⟩ is NOT the same as ⟨g, g⟩_θ. Let me be careful.\n\n⟨θg, g⟩ = ∫ (θg)(U) · g(U) dμ = ∫ g(θU) · g(U) dμ.\n\nThis is the reflection inner product ⟨g, g⟩_θ. And we need this to be ≥ 0.\n\nNow, the key insight from the transfer matrix approach: the transfer matrix T is NOT just any positive operator. It has a specific KERNEL structure that makes the reflection inner product positive.\n\nSpecifically, T = B*B where B is the \"half-step\" operator. Then:\n⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0  (standard inner product)\n\nBut we need ⟨θg, g⟩_θ ≥ 0, not ⟨g, Tg⟩ ≥ 0.\n\nHmm. Let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, I think the key is that the reflection positivity integral is NOT ⟨f, T^{2n} f⟩_θ. Let me reconsider the structure.\n\nThe full Boltzmann factor for T = 2n+1 time slices is:\nexp(-β S_total) = ∏_{t=0}^{2n-1} K_t\n\nwhere K_t is the transfer matrix kernel from time t to t+1. Each K_t integrates out the temporal links at time t.\n\nWait, actually the transfer matrix kernel K(u_s(t), u_s(t+1)) integrates out the temporal links between times t and t+1. So:\nexp(-β S_total) = ∏_{t=0}^{2n} K(u_s(t), u_s(t+1))\n\nwhere the product is over all time slices (with periodic BC, u_s(T) = u_s(0)).\n\nHmm, this isn't quite right either. Let me think more carefully.\n\nActually, the Wilson action is a sum over all plaquettes. Each plaquette is either:\n- Spatial plaquette (both directions spatial): involves only spatial links at one time\n- Temporal plaquette (one temporal, one spatial direction): involves temporal links at two times and spatial links at one time\n\nThe Boltzmann factor is:\nexp(-β S_total) = exp(-β S_spatial) · exp(-β S_temporal)\n\nwhere S_spatial = sum of spatial plaquettes, S_temporal = sum of temporal plaquettes.\n\nThe spatial part factorizes over time slices:\nexp(-β S_spatial) = ∏_t exp(-β S_spatial(t))\n\nThe temporal part couples adjacent time slices:\nexp(-β S_temporal) = ∏_t ∏_x B_p(u_t(x) · u_s(t,x) · u_t(x+1)⁻¹ · u_s(t+1,x)⁻¹)\n\nWait, I need to be more careful about the plaquette structure. Let me not get bogged down in details and think about the high-level structure.\n\nThe transfer matrix T has kernel:\nK(u_s, v_s) = exp(-β S_spatial(u_s)/2) · exp(-β S_spatial(v_s)/2) · ∫ ∏_x B_p(temporal plaquettes) dμ(u_t)\n\nThis integrates out the temporal links, giving a kernel from spatial links at time t to spatial links at time t+1.\n\nThe full path integral is:\nZ = Tr(T^{T-1})  (for periodic BC)\n\nAnd the reflection positivity integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ\n\nNow, the OS decomposition: with T = 2n+1, the reflection plane is at t = 0 (or t = n). The positive half is t = 0, ..., n and the negative half is t = n+1, ..., 2n.\n\nThe reflection θ maps t → -t (mod T). So t = 1 → t = 2n, t = 2 → t = 2n-1, etc.\n\nThe key: the transfer matrix T is self-adjoint (K(v, u) = conj(K(u, v))) and positive (as an operator).\n\nThe reflection positivity integral can be written as:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ = ⟨f, T^{2n} f⟩_θ\n\nWait, I keep going in circles. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that I need to understand the SPECIFIC factorization of the Boltzmann factor across the reflection plane.\n\nLet me look at the OS decomposition in the code. The action is decomposed as:\nS_total = S⁺ + S⁻ + S⁰\n\nwhere S⁺ = positive time plaquettes, S⁻ = negative time plaquettes, S⁰ = interface plaquettes.\n\nThe interface plaquettes are the temporal plaquettes at the reflection plane (t = 0). These involve temporal links at t = 0 and spatial links at t = 0.\n\nThe factorization is:\nexp(-β S_total) = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁻) · exp(-β S⁰)\n              = [exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2)] · [exp(-β S⁻) · exp(-β S⁰/2)]\n              = G_pos · G_neg\n\nwhere G_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2) and G_neg = exp(-β S⁻) · exp(-β S⁰/2).\n\nAnd G_neg(U) = G_pos(θU) (since S⁻(U) = S⁺(θU) and S⁰(θU) = S⁰(U)).\n\nSo the reflection positivity integral is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · G_pos(U) · G_pos(θU) dμ = ∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ\n\nwhere g = f · G_pos.\n\nNow, the key: G_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2). This is the Boltzmann factor for the positive half.\n\nThe positive half has n+1 time slices (t = 0, 1, ..., n). The transfer matrix T acts between adjacent time slices. So:\nG_pos = V^{1/2} · T^n\n\nwhere V^{1/2} = exp(-β S⁰/2) is the half-interface factor and T^n is the transfer matrix applied n times.\n\nWait, actually I need to be more careful. The transfer matrix T includes the spatial plaquette factors. Let me think about this.\n\nThe transfer matrix kernel is:\nK(u_s(t), u_s(t+1)) = exp(-β S_spatial(u_s(t))/2) · exp(-β S_spatial(u_s(t+1))/2) · ∫ ∏_x B_p(temporal plaquettes at t) dμ(u_t)\n\nSo T includes the spatial plaquette factors (split in half between adjacent time slices).\n\nThe positive half Boltzmann factor is:\nG_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2)\n\nS⁺ includes spatial plaquettes at t = 1, ..., n and temporal plaquettes at t = 0, ..., n-1 (between times 0-1, 1-2, ..., (n-1)-n).\n\nHmm, actually the spatial plaquettes at t = 0 are part of S⁰ (the interface), not S⁺. Let me check.\n\nActually, I think the interface S⁰ includes the temporal plaquettes at the reflection plane (t = 0), which involve temporal links at t = 0 and spatial links at t = 0 and t = 1 (or t = 0 and t = -1 = 2n).\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me look at the actual code to understand the decomposition.\n\nLet me look at the definitions of wilsonActionOSPositive, wilsonActionOSNegative, wilsonActionOSInterface, and the transfer matrix.\n\nActually, I think I should step back and think about the high-level strategy.\n\nThe key question is: can we prove ∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ ≥ 0 where g = f · G_pos?\n\nThe answer from the literature: YES, because G_pos has a specific product structure (product of transfer matrix kernels), and each transfer matrix kernel is PD.\n\nThe mechanism (Lüscher / Osterwalder-Seiler):\n1. G_pos = V^{1/2} · T^n (product of transfer matrix kernels)\n2. T is a positive operator (T = B*B, or T has PD kernel)\n3. The reflection inner product ⟨g, g⟩_θ = ⟨θg, g⟩ can be written as ⟨h, Th⟩ for some h, using the fact that T commutes with θ.\n\nWait, but I showed above that this reduces to ⟨θg, g⟩ which is NOT automatically ≥ 0.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. Maybe the key is that the reflection inner product IS positive semi-definite when restricted to functions of a specific form.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is different. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe reflection positivity is:\n⟨g, g⟩_θ = ∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ ≥ 0\n\nwhere g = f · G_pos and f depends only on positive spatial interface links.\n\nNow, G_pos = exp(-β S⁺) · exp(-β S⁰/2). The key: G_pos is a PRODUCT of PD functions (plaquette factors), and the product is over plaquettes in the positive half.\n\nThe reflection positivity follows from the fact that each plaquette factor is PD, and the product structure across the reflection plane preserves positivity.\n\nBut session 34 found that the character expansion gives ∑ A² (not ∑ |A|²), which is an obstacle. And session 35 found that the single-step kernel IS PD via gauge-fixing.\n\nSo the gauge-fixing approach AVOIDS the ∑ A² obstacle. The question is: can we use the gauge-fixing approach for the FULL reflection positivity (not just the single-step kernel)?\n\nLet me think about this. The single-step kernel is PD because:\nK_temporal(u_s, v_s) = ∑_γ (|c_γ|²/d_γ) χ_γ(W(0)·W(1)) ≥ 0\n\nThis is PD as a function of (u_s, v_s). The key: |c_γ|²/d_γ ≥ 0 and χ_γ is PD.\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral is:\n∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ\n\nwhere g = f · G_pos. If G_pos = V^{1/2} · T^n, then:\n∫ g(U) · g(θU) dμ = ∫ f(U) · V^{1/2}(U) · T^n(U) · f(θU) · V^{1/2}(θU) · T^n(θU) dμ\n\nUsing T commutes with θ: T^n(θU) = θ(T^n(U)).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about the simplest case: n = 0 (T = 1, single time slice).\n\nWith T = 1 (one time slice), there are no temporal plaquettes (the temporal direction has only one slice). So S_total = S_spatial only. And S⁺ = S⁻ = 0, S⁰ = S_spatial.\n\nWait, but with T = 1, the reflection θ maps t = 0 to t = 0 (since -0 = 0 mod 1). So θ is the identity on time, but it inverts temporal links and reflects spatial links.\n\nHmm, with T = 1, the spatial links at t = 0 are the only links. The reflection θ inverts temporal links (but there are none with T = 1... actually with periodic BC, the temporal link at t = 0 connects t = 0 to t = 0, so it's a loop). And θ reflects spatial links (x → -x).\n\nThis is a degenerate case. Let me think about T = 3 (n = 1).\n\nWith T = 3, the time slices are t = 0, 1, 2. The reflection θ maps t = 1 → t = 2, t = 0 → t = 0. The positive half is t = 0, 1 and the negative half is t = 2.\n\nThe interface is at t = 0. The interface plaquettes involve temporal links at t = 0 (connecting t = 0 to t = 1 and t = 0 to t = 2 = -1).\n\nS⁺ = plaquettes at t = 1 (spatial) + temporal plaquettes between t = 0 and t = 1, and between t = 1 and t = 2.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me just look at the code to understand the decomposition.\n\nActually, let me take a step back. I think the most productive thing to do is:\n\n1. Search for the actual proof mechanism in the literature (Seiler 1982, BFS 1979).\n2. Look at the existing code infrastructure.\n3. Based on that, decide on a concrete plan.\n\nLet me search for the literature and look at the code in parallel.\n\nLet me search for \"Osterwalder Seiler reflection positivity lattice gauge\" and \"Brydges Fröhlich Seiler half gauge fields\". And let me look at the transfer matrix construction in the code.\n\nActually, I realize the literature search via arxiv/web might not find these old papers (1978-1982). Let me try web search for these specific papers.\n\nLet me also look at the key code: the transfer matrix construction, the OS decomposition, and the existing infrastructure.\n\nLet me be efficient. Let me search the web for the key references and look at the code.","turn":14,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":31,"role":"Tool","content":"Search results (8):\n\n1. [https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1978AnPhy.110..440O/abstract]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - NASA/ADS\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n2. [https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0003491678900398]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - ScienceDirect\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n3. [https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Gauge-field-theories-on-a-lattice-Osterwalder-Seiler/610728f713f24d54488c5e5be85d5d96092211e3]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - Semantic Scholar\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n4. [https://archive.org/download/wikipedia-scholarly-sources-corpus/10.1016%252F0001-4575%252871%252990016-9.zip/10.1016%252F0003-4916%252878%252990039-8.pdf]\n   PDF PII: 0003-4916(78)90039-8 - Archive.org\n   We begin a rigorous, nonperturbative investigation of quantum field theories with local internal symmetries. We discuss the lattice approximation of Yang-Mills fields and offer- mion fields in the Euclidean setup and we verify physical positivity for the Schwinger functions of these approximations. This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence ...\n\n5. [https://rd.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF01221251.pdf]\n   PDF General proof of Osterwalder-Schrader positivity for the Wilson action\n   Abstract. We extend theproof byOsterwalder and Seiler ofreflection positivity of lattice gauge theories with fermions tthe case inwhich t reflection e is performed with respect toplanes containing sites. Our proof applies toall observables and relies on the particular structure of the Wilson action. This assures the positivity of the correlation functions (O(F)F) for any gauge invariant ...\n\n6. [https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-63/issue-1/On-confinement-of-fermions-in-strongly-coupled-lattice/cmp/1103904441.pdf]\n   PDF On Confinement of Fermions in Strongly Coupled Lattice Gauge Theory\n   rmulation and to the proof of convergence of the cluster expansion. As usually, once the convergence is proven, existence of the exponentially clustering infinite volume theory follows and the standard construction [11] gives the physical Hubert space and the transfer matrix e\"2Π, since the Osterwalder-Schrader positivity holds, a pro XDr. (1)\n\n7. [https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-113/issue-3/General-proof-of-Osterwalder-Schrader-positivity-for-the-Wilson-action/cmp/1104160284.pdf]\n   PDF General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity for the Wilson Action\n   Abstract We extend the proof by Osterwalder and Seiler of reflection positivity of lattice gauge theories with fermions to the case in which the reflection is performed with respect to planes containing sites. Our proof applies to all observables and relies on the particular structure of the Wilson action. This assures the positivity of the correlation functions\n\n8. [https://www.bibsonomy.org/bibtex/12499deda4efb08cf6df8a06f0b844464?lang=en]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - BibSonomy\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories. BibTeX key\n\n\nTime: 2026-08-06 04:19:18 UTC","timestamp":1785989958,"token_count":1079,"tool_call_id":"c73d6927-0500-40c9-9639-122d88bf5265","full_token_estimate":894,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"web_search","file_path":null,"old_text":"Osterwalder Seiler 1978 reflection positivity lattice gauge field theory transfer matrix proof mechanism","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":4304,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1104,"edit_line":null},"turn":14,"is_prune_marker":false}
