e to give positivity.\n\nLet me think about this. The fullReflect form (after σ-disappearance) is:\nC · ∑_w F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*}(u⁰) · c_w(u⁰) dμ⁰\n\nThe gauge-fixing approach shows that the single-step kernel is:\nK_temporal = ∑_γ (|c_γ|²/d_γ) χ_γ ≥ 0\n\nThe key: |c_γ|² = c_γ · conj(c_γ). In the fullReflect form, we have c_{w*} · c_w. If c_{w*} = conj(c_w) (dual pairing), then c_{w*} · c_w = |c_w|² ≥ 0.\n\nFrom session 17 (item 71), fourierCoeffNeg = fourierCoeffPos(w*). And the dual pairing gives A_{dual(α)} = conj(A_α). So c_{w*} = conj(c_w) if the dual pairing holds.\n\nBut session 34 (item 89) found that the dual pairing only helps for self-dual characters; non-self-dual terms can be negative. And the CG decomposition gives C_{α,j,i} ≠ conj(C_{α,i,j}).\n\nHmm, so the dual pairing doesn't fully solve the problem.\n\nOK, I think I need to take a step back. Let me think about what the gauge-fixing approach actually gives and how it relates to the fullReflect form.\n\nThe gauge-fixing approach (session 35) shows that the single-step transfer matrix kernel is PD:\nK_temporal(u_s, v_s) = ∑_γ (|c_γ|²/d_γ) χ_γ(W(0)·W(1))\n\nwhere W(x) = v_s(x) · u_s(x)⁻¹.\n\nThis is PD because |c_γ|²/d_γ ≥ 0 and χ_γ is PD.\n\nThe key: the gauge-fixing approach uses the SQUARE of the character expansion coefficients (|c_γ|²), not the coefficients themselves (c_γ). This avoids the ∑ A² obstacle.\n\nNow, the fullReflect form involves c_{w*} · c_w (product of Fourier coefficients at dual indices). If c_{w*} = conj(c_w), then c_{w*} · c_w = |c_w|² ≥ 0. But this is NOT always true (session 34).\n\nThe gauge-fixing approach avoids this by using |c_γ|² directly (from Schur orthogonality), not c_{w*} · c_w.\n\nSo the gauge-fixing approach is fundamentally different from the character expansion approach. It doesn't go through the fullReflect form.\n\nOK, I think the key insight is:\n\n1. The character expansion approach (fullReflect form) gives c_{w*} · c_w, which is NOT obviously ≥ 0 (session 34 obstacle).\n2. The gauge-fixing approach gives |c_γ|²/d_γ ≥ 0, which IS ≥ 0 (session 35 breakthrough).\n3. The gauge-fixing approach works for the SINGLE-STEP kernel, not the full reflection positivity.\n4. The full reflection positivity requires the Lüscher decomposition (T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2}) to reduce to single-step positivity.\n\nSo the plan is:\n1. Formalize the single-step kernel PD property (gauge-fixing approach).\n2. Use the Lüscher decomposition to reduce reflection positivity to single-step positivity.\n\nBut this is a LOT of work. Let me think about whether there's a shortcut.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the σ-disappearance. If fourierCoeffPos(w*, σ(u⁰)) = fourierCoeffPos(w*, u⁰), then the fullReflect form becomes:\nC · ∑_w F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*}(u⁰) · c_w(u⁰) dμ⁰\n\nNow, this is a QUADRATIC FORM in the Fourier coefficients c_w. The matrix is:\nM_{w, w'} = F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · δ_{w', w*} dμ⁰\n\nWait, no. The sum is over w, and the integrand is c_{w*}(u⁰) · c_w(u⁰). This is NOT a standard quadratic form ∑_{w,w'} M_{w,w'} c_w c_{w'}. It's ∑_w F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*} · c_w dμ⁰.\n\nLet me reindex: let w' = w*. Then w = w'* (since fullReflectReindex is an involution). So:\n∑_w F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*} · c_w dμ⁰ = ∑_{w'} F(w'*) · ∫ charFactorInt(w'*, u⁰) · c_{w'} · c_{w'*} dμ⁰\n\nSo the sum is:\n∑_w [F(w) · charFactorInt(w, u⁰) + F(w*) · charFactorInt(w*, u⁰)] / 2 · c_{w*} · c_w dμ⁰\n\nWait, no. The sum over w and the reindexed sum over w' = w* give the same terms (just reordered). So:\n∑_w F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*} · c_w dμ⁰ = ∑_w F(w*) · ∫ charFactorInt(w*, u⁰) · c_w · c_{w*} dμ⁰\n\nThese are equal (just reindexing). So the sum is:\n∑_w F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*} · c_w dμ⁰\n\nThis is a sum over w of F(w) · ∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · c_{w*}(u⁰) · c_w(u⁰) dμ⁰.\n\nFor this to be ≥ 0, we need... hmm, it's not a standard PSD condition. It's a sum of products