mporal links at positive times. So we can't just integrate out all temporal links.\n\nHmm, this complicates things. The transfer matrix approach integrates out temporal links, but f depends on them.\n\nWait, but the transfer matrix approach works differently. The transfer matrix T_true maps spatial links at time t to spatial links at time t+1. The function f is a function of ALL links (spatial and temporal) at positive times. So f is NOT just a function of spatial links.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. The transfer matrix approach requires f to be a function of spatial links only (at each time slice). But f depends on temporal links too.\n\nActually, I think the key is that the transfer matrix approach works at the level of the PATH INTEGRAL, not the observable. The path integral is:\nZ = ∫ exp(-β S_total) dμ = Tr(T_true^{T-1})\n\nAnd the expectation of an observable f is:\n⟨f⟩ = ∫ f(U) · exp(-β S_total) dμ / Z\n\nThe reflection positivity is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ ≥ 0\n\nNow, f depends on both spatial and temporal links. But the transfer matrix integrates out temporal links. So we need to express f in terms of spatial links only.\n\nThe key: f can be expanded in terms of temporal link characters (Peter-Weyl). Each term in the expansion is a function of spatial links only (times a character of temporal links). The transfer matrix then acts on each term.\n\nBut this is the character expansion approach, which has the ∑ A² obstacle (session 34).\n\nHmm. So the transfer matrix approach requires expanding f in temporal link characters, which brings back the character expansion obstacle.\n\nOK, I think I'm going in circles. Let me take a completely different approach.\n\nLet me re-read the key insight from session 35: the σ twist is an ARTIFACT of the reduction to ∫ F·F(σ) dμ. The original path integral has INDEPENDENT bra and ket.\n\nSo maybe the approach is: DON'T reduce to ∫ F·F(σ) dμ. Instead, work with the INDEPENDENT bra and ket.\n\nThe reflection positivity is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ ≥ 0\n\nThe key: f(U) depends on positive-time links and f(θU) depends on negative-time links (reflected). The Boltzmann factor exp(-β S_total) couples all links.\n\nNow, the INDEPENDENT bra and ket approach: instead of f(U) · f(θU), consider f(U⁺) · g(U⁻) where U⁺ and U⁻ are independent. The reflection positivity is the special case g = θf.\n\nBut the key insight is that the transfer matrix T_true maps U⁺ to U⁻ (through the interface). So:\n∫ f(U⁺) · g(U⁻) · exp(-β S_total) dμ = ⟨f, T_true^n · V · T_true^n · g⟩\n\nwhere T_true is the true transfer matrix and V is the interface operator.\n\nFor the reflection positivity, g = θf. So:\n∫ f(U⁺) · θf(U⁻) · exp(-β S_total) dμ = ⟨f, T_true^n · V · T_true^n · θf⟩\n\nUsing T_true commuting with θ:\n= ⟨f, T_true^n · V · θ · T_true^n · f⟩\n= ⟨f, T_true^n · V · θ · T_true^n · f⟩\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. And it still involves θ.\n\nOK, I think I need to accept that this is a deep problem and the formalization will require significant effort. Let me document my findings and plan the next steps.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time. The key insight from session 35 is that the single-step kernel IS PD. The single-step kernel is:\nK_temporal(u_s, v_s) = ∑_γ (|c_γ|²/d_γ) χ_γ(W(0)·W(1))\n\nThis is PD as a function of (u_s, v_s). The PD property means:\n∫∫ f(u_s) · conj(f(v_s)) · K_temporal(u_s, v_s) dμ(u_s) dμ(v_s) ≥ 0\n\nfor all f. But the reflection positivity is:\n∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β S_total) dμ ≥ 0\n\nThese are DIFFERENT. The PD property is a double integral with conj(f), while reflection positivity is a single integral with f(θU).\n\nSo the PD of the single-step kernel does NOT directly imply reflection positivity. We need the Lüscher decomposition to connect them.\n\nOK, I think I've done enough analysis. Let me document my findings and plan the next steps.\n\nThe key findings:\n1. The σ twist DISAPPEARS from the fullReflect form when using g_posInterface_sigma_