ilson action\" paper (Lüscher?) seem most relevant. Let me fetch the Lüscher-style general proof.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about the overall strategy. The context is at 83%. I should:\n1. Do focused analysis this session\n2. Prepare a good handoff\n\nLet me look at the key result `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` more carefully — it reduces the reflection positivity integral to `∫ g·(Tg)`. If T is a POSITIVE operator, then `∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0` trivially. So the KEY is to prove T is positive.\n\nBut wait — `transferMatrixCorrect` is the transfer matrix that integrates out the NEGATIVE links. Is it positive? The session 35 breakthrough says the single-step kernel IS PD. But `transferMatrixCorrect` here is NOT the single-step kernel — it's the kernel that includes the σ twist (via `reflectToPosInterface`).\n\nActually, let me re-read. `transferMatrixCorrect` (line 1279):\n```\ntransferMatrixCorrect ψ u = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, restrictToInterface(u))) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(extend(U⁻,u))/2 + S_int(extend(U⁻,u)))) dμ⁻\n```\n\nAnd `transferMatrixReflected` (line 2459) is the change-of-variables version:\n```\ntransferMatrixReflected ψ u = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, σ(restrictToInterface(u)))) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(merge(V⁺,σ(u⁰)))/2 + S_int(extend(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) dμ⁺\n```\n\nThese are equal (transferMatrix_change_of_variables). The reflected version has the σ twist explicitly.\n\nSo `∫ g·(Tg) = ∫ g·(T_reflected g)`. The reflected version has σ. This is the obstacle.\n\nNow, the session 35 insight: the σ twist is an artifact. The ORIGINAL transfer matrix `transferMatrixCorrect` (before the change of variables) does NOT have σ — it has `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)` which reflects the negative config to positive. \n\nHmm, but `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)` — what does this do? It takes a negative config U⁻ and reflects it to the positive region. This is the reflection map. So the transfer matrix kernel is:\n```\nK(u, v) = ∫_{U⁻} [ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) = ψ at reflected point] · exp(...) dμ⁻\n```\n\nActually the transfer matrix T acts on functions ψ of PosInterfaceConfig. The kernel is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫ K(u, v) ψ(v) dν(v)\n```\n\nLet me think about whether `transferMatrixCorrect` is positive. \n\nActually, the cleanest way: `∫ g·(Tg) = ∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀` (proven). And `∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀` — if we DON'T do the change of variables that introduces σ, can we see it's ≥ 0?\n\n`∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀` where G(U) = f(U)·exp(-βS⁺(U))·exp(-βS_int(U)/2).\n\nHmm. The issue is G(θU) = f(θU)·exp(-βS⁺(θU))·exp(-βS_int(θU)/2) = f(θU)·exp(-βS⁻(U))·exp(-βS_int(U)/2).\n\nSo `∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β(S⁺+S⁻+S_int)) dμ₀ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-βS_W(U)) dμ₀`.\n\nThis is the reflection positivity integral. The σ twist appears when we try to factor this through the transfer matrix.\n\nOK here's the key question for the proof strategy. The session 35 breakthrough is about the SINGLE-STEP transfer matrix being PD. But our `transferMatrixCorrect` is the FULL transfer matrix (integrating out ALL negative links, not just one time step). \n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. The lattice has T time steps (T odd, periodic). The \"transfer matrix\" in the Osterwalder-Seiler sense is the single-step operator that propagates one time slice. The reflection positivity for the FULL lattice involves T^{2n} or similar.\n\nBut in our formalization, `transferMatrixCorrect` integrates out ALL negative-time links at once. This is NOT the single-step transfer matrix. It's more like the \"half-lattice\" operator.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me focus on what's concretely actionable.\n\nThe most promising approach from session 35: formalize the single-step kernel PD property, then reduce reflection positivity to it. But this is a BIG undertaking.\n\nLet me reconsider the actual structure. The key proven result is:\n```\n∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g·(Tg) dμ⁺⁰   (integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg)\n```\nwhere T = transferMatrixCorrect. If T is positive (as an operator), we're done.\n\nIs transferMatrixCorrect positive? Let me think about its kernel. \n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(extend(U⁻,u))/2 + S_int(extend(U⁻,u)))) dμ⁻\n```\n\nThe kernel K(u, v) such that (Tψ)(u) = ∫ K(u,v) ψ(v) dν(v):\n- v = reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰) for some U⁻\n- The map U⁻ ↦ v = reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰) — is this a bijection in U⁻ for fixed u⁰? reflectToPosInterface reflects U⁻ to positive and keeps u⁰. So v's positive part = reflect(U⁻), v's interface part = u⁰. So for fixed u⁰, U⁻ ↦ v is a bijection (reflection is a bijection). \n\nSo (Tψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(extend(reflect⁻¹(V⁺), u))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺ (after change of variables U⁻ ↦ V⁺ = reflect(U⁻)).\n\nWait, this is exactly transferMatrixReflected but WITHOUT the σ! Let me re-examine. transferMatrixReflected has `σ(restrictToInterface(u))` = σ(u⁰). But transferMatrixCorrect has `restrictToInterface(u)` = u⁰ (no σ).\n\nHmm wait. Let me re-read transferMatrixCorrect:\n```\nψ (reflectToPosInterface N T L U_minus (restrictToInterface N T L u))\n```\nSo it's `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)` — no σ on u⁰.\n\nAnd transferMatrixReflected:\n```\nψ (mergePosInterface N T L V_plus (sigmaInterface N T L (restrictToInterface N T L u)))\n```\nSo it's `merge(V⁺, σ(u⁰))` — WITH σ on u⁰.\n\nAnd `transferMatrix_change_of_variables` says these are EQUAL. How? Because `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰) = merge(reflect(U⁻), σ(u⁰))` (this is `reflectToPosInterface_involution`-like). \n\nSo the change of variables U⁻ ↦ V⁺ = reflect(U⁻) introduces σ(u⁰) on the interface. This is the σ twist!\n\nSo in the `transferMatrixCorrect` form (before CoV), the kernel is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · W(U⁻, u) dμ⁻\n```\nwhere reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰) = merge(reflect(U⁻), σ(u⁰)).\n\nSo even in the \"original\" form, the σ is there (hidden in reflectToPosInterface). The σ twist is NOT an artifact of the CoV — it's intrinsic to the reflection.\n\nOK so this confirms the σ twist is fundamental. The session 35 \"artifact\" claim needs refinement.\n\nLet me reconsider. The reflection positivity `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-βS_W) dμ₀`. The reflection θ maps positive↔negative AND inverts temporal interface links (σ). The σ is part of θ.\n\nSo the question is: is `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-βS_W) dμ₀ ≥ 0` for f depending only on positive+spatial-interface links?\n\nThis is EXACTLY the Osterwalder-Seiler theorem. The proof in the literature (Lüscher's \"General proof\" paper, and Osterwalder-Seiler) uses the factorization of the Wilson action and the PD of the plaquette factor.\n\nLet me look at the Lüscher general proof paper more carefully — it's the most directly relevant. Let me fetch it.\n\nActually, I already have the PDF but it's garbled (binary). Let me search for the actual proof mechanism.\n\nThe key result from the \"General proof of Osterwalder-Schrader positivity for the Wilson action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1988) — this is by Lüscher. The proof relies on:\n1. The Wilson action is a sum of plaquette contributions.\n2. Each plaquette factor exp(-β·(1 - Re Tr U_p)/N) is a PD function (class function, PD).\n3. The reflection positivity follows from the factorization and PD of plaquette factors.\n\nThe KEY mechanism: Write the Boltzmann factor as a product over plaquettes. Split plaquettes into \"positive\" (in the positive half), \"negative\" (in the negative half), and \"interface\" (straddling the reflection plane). \n\n- Positive plaquettes: depend on positive links → factor exp(-βS⁺) goes with f(U).\n- Negative plaquettes: by reflection symmetry, exp(-βS⁻(U)) = exp(-βS⁺(θU)) → goes with f(θU).\n- Interface plaquettes: straddle the plane. These are the tricky ones.\n\nFor the interface plaquettes, the proof uses the fact that the plaquette factor is a PD class function, and expands it in characters. The character expansion + the reflection gives a sum of |Fourier coeff|² terms.\n\nBut session 34 found that the character expansion gives ∑A² not ∑|A|², which is the obstacle. And session 35 found the single-step kernel IS PD via gauge-fixing.\n\nHmm. Let me look at the actual Lüscher proof mechanism. The key is the \"half-plaquette\" or \"half-gauge-field\" decomposition.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach, given the codebase state, is:\n\nThe proven result `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg)` reduces reflection positivity to T being positive. And `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` gives the character expansion of `∫ g·(Tg)`:\n```\nComplex.ofReal(∫ ψ·Tψ) = C·∑_w F(w)·∫ charFactorInt(w, u⁰)·A_{w*}(σ(u⁰))·A_w(u⁰) du⁰\n```\n\nFor this to be ≥ 0, we need the integrand `charFactorInt(w,u⁰)·A_{w*}(σ(u⁰))·A_w(u⁰)` to integrate to something ≥ 0 (after summing over w).\n\nThe session 34 obstacle: A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) ≠ conj(A_w(u⁰)) in general, so this is NOT |A_w|².\n\nBUT — session 35's insight about gauge-fixing might give a way to show the SUM over w is ≥ 0 even if individual terms aren't.\n\nThis is getting deep. Given I'm at 83% context, let me:\n1. Fetch the Lüscher general proof paper (the most relevant one) to understand the actual mechanism.\n2. Document findings and prepare handoff.\n\nLet me try to fetch the Lüscher paper text. The projecteuclid URL gave garbled PDF. Let me try a different approach — search for the proof mechanism.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole situation strategically. The codebase has:\n- `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`: ∫G·G(θU) = ∫g·(Tg) [needs dependsOnlyOnPosInterface]\n- `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect`: character expansion of ∫g·(Tg)\n\nThe axiom `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` is EXACTLY `∫ G·G(θU) ≥ 0` with `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface`.\n\nSo to close the axiom, I need to prove `∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0` (via integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg, but note it needs dependsOnlyOnPosInterface not the spatial version — there's a mismatch! The axiom uses dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface but integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg uses dependsOnlyOnPosInterface).\n\nWait, let me check. The axiom (line 2476-2478) uses `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface`. And `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` (line 4938) uses `dependsOnlyOnPosInterface`. And `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface` implies `dependsOnlyOnPosInterface` (from session 30, item 85).\n\nSo the chain would be:\n1. `∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g·(Tg) dμ⁺⁰` (integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg, using dependsOnlyOnPosInterface which follows from dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface)\n2. `∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0` (need to prove T positive)\n\nSo the crux is step 2: prove T = transferMatrixCorrect is a positive operator.\n\nNow, is T positive? T integrates out negative links with the reflection. The kernel involves reflectToPosInterface which has σ. \n\nHmm, but actually — maybe T is NOT obviously positive, and the right approach is different.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually, maybe the right framing is: the reflection positivity `∫ f·f(θ)·exp(-βS_W) ≥ 0` should be proven DIRECTLY via the plaquette factorization + PD, WITHOUT going through the transfer matrix. The transfer matrix approach introduces σ. The direct approach (Osterwalder-Seiler / Lüscher) avoids it.\n\nThe direct approach: \n`∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-βS_W(U)) dμ₀`\n= `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·∏_p exp(-β·plaquetteContribution(U_p)) dμ₀`\n\nSplit plaquettes: positive half P⁺, negative half P⁻, interface P⁰.\n- For p ∈ P⁻: U_p(θU) = θ(U_p) and plaquetteContribution is a class function invariant under θ, so exp(-β·c(U_p)) for p∈P⁻ depends on negative links. By reflection, ∏_{p∈P⁻} exp(-βc(U_p)) = ∏_{p∈P⁺} exp(-βc(θU_p))... \n\nHmm, this is the standard argument. The interface plaquettes are the issue. For the interface, each plaquette straddles the plane and involves one temporal link AT the interface (t=0, μ=0). \n\nThe Lüscher/Osterwalder-Seiler trick for interface plaquettes: the interface plaquette factor is a PD function of the SPATIAL interface links (after integrating/gauge-fixing the temporal interface link). OR: expand the interface plaquette factor in characters and use orthogonality.\n\nThis is exactly what `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` does — it expands the interface Boltzmann factor in characters. And the obstacle (session 34) is that this gives ∑A² not ∑|A|².\n\nSo the character expansion approach has a fundamental obstacle. The gauge-fixing approach (session 35) is the alternative.\n\nOK. I think I've done enough analysis. The key realization: the σ twist is intrinsic (not an artifact), and the character expansion gives ∑A² not ∑|A|². The gauge-fixing approach for the single-step kernel is PD, but reducing the full reflection positivity to it is non-trivial.\n\nLet me fetch the Lüscher paper to understand the actual mechanism, then document and handoff. Let me try fetching a readable version.\n\nActually, given context constraints, let me be strategic. Let me look at whether there's a simpler path: maybe the interface plaquette factor, when f depends only on SPATIAL interface links (not temporal), the temporal interface links can be integrated out giving a PD kernel.\n\nThe key: `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface` means f does NOT depend on temporal interface links (μ=0, t=0). So the temporal interface links are \"free\" to be integrated out. \n\nThe interface action S_int involves plaquettes that use temporal interface links. If we integrate out the temporal interface links FIRST (before the character expansion), we get a kernel that's a function of spatial interface links only. And THIS kernel might be PD (via the gauge-fixing + character expansion = single-step kernel PD from session 35).\n\nThis is the Lüscher mechanism! Integrate out temporal interface links → get PD kernel on spatial interface links → reflection positivity follows.\n\nSo the proof structure:\n1. `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-βS_W) dμ₀` — f depends on positive + spatial-interface links.\n2. The temporal interface links (μ=0, t=0) appear ONLY in S_int (interface plaquettes) and NOT in f.\n3. Integrate out temporal interface links: `∫_{u⁰_t} exp(-βS_int) du⁰_t` = K(u⁰_s, ...) — a kernel.\n4. This kernel K is PD (via gauge-fixing + character expansion, the session 35 breakthrough).\n5. The remaining integral `∫ f·f(θ)·exp(-βS⁺)·exp(-βS⁻)·K dμ'` is then ≥ 0 by PD of K and the reflection structure.\n\nThis is the Lüscher decomposition T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} mentioned in the codebase comments!\n\nSo the concrete next step: formalize that integrating out temporal interface links gives a PD kernel. This is the \"Lüscher mechanism\" and it's the session 35 breakthrough applied to the interface.\n\nLet me verify this is consistent with the codebase. The `transferMatrixReflected` integrates over V⁺ (positive links) and has σ(u⁰) on the interface. If instead we integrate over u⁰_t (temporal interface) first...\n\nActually, I realize the codebase's `transferMatrixCorrect` integrates over U⁻ (ALL negative links), not just temporal interface. The Lüscher approach would integrate out temporal interface links specifically.\n\nThis is a significant restructuring. Given context limits, let me document this analysis and prepare a handoff with the concrete plan.\n\nLet me fetch the Lüscher paper to confirm the mechanism, then write up the analysis in the design doc and handoff.\n\nLet me try to get the Lüscher paper content via a search for the proof.","turn":26,"is_prune_marker":false}
