)\n- `g_posInterface` (line 1327): g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S⁺/2)\n- `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` (line 4936): ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) — THIS IS KEY\n- `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` (line 5792): the fullReflect form\n- `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible` (line 1986): σ disappears from g\n- `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` (PositiveDefiniteIntegral.lean:1196): shared-variable positivity\n\nThe key relationship is: `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` shows that ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) where T = transferMatrixCorrect. So if we can show T is a POSITIVE operator (i.e., ∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0 for all g), then reflection positivity follows.\n\nBut wait — the axiom `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` is about ∫ G·G(θU) ≥ 0, and `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` reduces this to ∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0. So the question is: is T = transferMatrixCorrect a positive operator?\n\nLet me look at what `transferMatrixCorrect` actually is. From line 1279:\n```\ntransferMatrixCorrect(ψ, u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, restrictToInterface(u))) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(extendToFullConfig(U⁻, u))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(U⁻, u)))) ∂μ⁻\n```\n\nSo T(ψ)(u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · K(U⁻, u) ∂μ⁻ where K is the Boltzmann factor.\n\nThe key insight from session 35: the single-step transfer matrix kernel IS PD. But the σ twist is the obstacle.\n\nWait, but `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` already handles the σ twist! It shows ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg). So if T is positive, we're done.\n\nThe question is: is T positive? T(ψ)(u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) ∂μ⁻.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The transfer matrix T acts on functions on PosInterfaceConfig. The kernel is:\nT(ψ)(u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) ∂μ⁻\n\nUsing the change of variables U⁻ ↦ V⁺ = reflectNegToPos(U⁻) (which is measure-preserving, proven in `transferMatrix_change_of_variables`), this becomes:\nT(ψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) ∂μ⁺\n\nThis is `transferMatrixReflected`. The σ twist appears here: ψ is evaluated at mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰)), not mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰).\n\nBut with `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface`, ψ = g_posInterface doesn't depend on the temporal interface links (only spatial). And `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible` shows that g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) = g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)). So the σ twist DISAPPEARS for g!\n\nThis is the KEY INSIGHT. Let me re-read the proof structure:\n\n1. `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`: ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) where g = g_posInterface and T = transferMatrixCorrect.\n2. `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible`: g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) = g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) — σ disappears from g.\n3. `transferMatrix_change_of_variables`: T = transferMatrixReflected (change of variables U⁻ → V⁺).\n\nSo T(g)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·(...)) ∂μ⁺\n           = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) ∂μ⁺  (by σ-invisible)\n\nWait, but the exp factor still has σ in it through S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)). Does σ disappear from the Boltzmann factor too?\n\nActually, let me re-read `transferMatrixReflected` more carefully:\n```\ntransferMatrixReflected(ψ, u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) ∂μ⁺\n```\n\nThe S_int term: `wilsonActionOSInterface(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u))`. This depends on u (the full PosInterfaceConfig, including u⁰). Does σ affect this?\n\nFrom session 33, `osPositiveOfPosInterface_sigma_invariant` shows S⁺ ignores σ. But what about S_int? The interface action S_int involves the temporal interface links. Let me check if S_int is σ-invariant.\n\nActually, from the reflection positivity setup, `interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic` shows S_int(θU) = S_int(U). But σ is NOT the same as θ. σ = sigmaInterface only inverts the temporal interface links (μ=0 at t=0), while θ = reflectLinkVariable inverts everything.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The key question is: after the σ-invisibility of g, does the transfer matrix T become a POSITIVE operator?\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what \"positive operator\" means here. T is positive if ∫ ψ·(Tψ) ≥ 0 for all ψ. \n\nWith the σ-invisibility, for ψ = g (depending only on pos+spatial-interface):\nT(g)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) ∂μ⁺\n\nWait, I used σ(u⁰) → u⁰ via σ-invisibility for g, but the exp factor still has σ(u⁰) in the S⁺ term. But `osPositiveOfPosInterface_sigma_invariant` says S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) = S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)). So the S⁺ term also loses σ.\n\nWhat about S_int? `wilsonActionOSInterface(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u))` — this depends on u (the full config including u⁰). Does σ affect S_int here?\n\nActually, I think the key point is that S_int involves plaquettes at the interface, which couple positive-time links, negative-time links, and interface links. The reflectPosToNeg(V⁺) gives the negative-time links, and u gives the positive+interface links. The S_int term involves temporal interface links u⁰_t.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me look at the actual structure of the proof more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the most productive thing to do right now is to:\n1. Search for the Seiler lecture notes online (the most accessible source)\n2. Look at the existing infrastructure more carefully to understand what's proven and what's missing\n\nLet me search for the Seiler lecture notes and also look at the key lemma `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` which was proven in session 32.\n\nActually, I'm at 83% context. I should be strategic. Let me focus on:\n1. Understanding the exact gap between what's proven and what's needed\n2. Searching for the Seiler notes\n3. Documenting findings and handing off\n\nLet me look at the key question: after `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` reduces ∫ G·G(θU) to ∫ g·(Tg), what's needed to show ∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0?\n\nThe answer from session 35: T needs to be a positive operator. The single-step kernel IS PD (via gauge-fixing + character expansion). But the σ twist was the obstacle.\n\nBUT — with `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface` and `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible`, the σ twist DISAPPEARS from g. So the question is: does the σ twist also disappear from the Boltzmann factor in T?\n\nLet me check: in `transferMatrixReflected`, the Boltzmann factor is:\nexp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u))))\n\nAfter σ-invisibility of g and S⁺:\nexp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u))))\n\nThe S_int term still depends on u (which includes u⁰). But u here is the ORIGINAL u, not σ(u⁰). So there's no σ in the S_int term — it's just S_int evaluated at the original u.\n\nWait, but the S_int term is `wilsonActionOSInterface(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u))`. Here u is the PosInterfaceConfig (positive + interface links). The extendToFullConfig combines reflectPosToNeg(V⁺) (negative links) with u (positive + interface links). So S_int is evaluated at a full config with:\n- Positive links from u\n- Negative links from reflectPosToNeg(V⁺) \n- Interface links from u (including temporal u⁰_t)\n\nSo S_int depends on u⁰_t (the temporal interface links). There's no σ twist here — it's the original u⁰_t.\n\nSo after σ-invisibility, T(g)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) ∂μ⁺\n\nThis is a kernel K(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) integrated against g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)).\n\nHmm, but this is still complicated. The kernel involves S_int which couples V⁺ (through reflectPosToNeg) with u.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The key insight from session 35 is that the SINGLE-STEP transfer matrix kernel is PD. The \"single-step\" means one time step. The full transfer matrix T is a product of single-step transfer matrices.\n\nBut actually, in the lattice gauge theory, the transfer matrix T for the full lattice is already a single \"time step\" — it maps from one time slice to the next. The full partition function is a product of T's (one for each time step), but the reflection positivity only needs ONE T (or T² for the reflection).\n\nWait, I need to be more careful. Let me re-read the structure.\n\nThe reflection positivity is: ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0 where G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_int(U)/2).\n\nThis is reduced (via `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`) to: ∫ g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ_{pos+int} ≥ 0.\n\nSo we need T to be positive. T is the transfer matrix that integrates out the negative-time links. \n\nNow, the KEY QUESTION: is T positive?\n\nFrom the Osterwalder-Seiler theorem, T IS positive. The proof uses the gauge-fixing + character expansion mechanism from session 35.\n\nBut the σ twist was the obstacle. However, with `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface`, the σ twist disappears from g (via `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible`). \n\nSo the question becomes: does the σ twist disappear from the KERNEL of T?\n\nLet me look at this more carefully. After the change of variables (transferMatrix_change_of_variables), T = transferMatrixReflected:\nT(ψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) ∂μ⁺\n\nFor ψ = g (σ-invisible):\nT(g)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)))) ∂μ⁺\n\nNow, the kernel is:\nK(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2 + S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u))))\n\nAnd T(g)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) ∂μ⁺\n\nFor T to be positive, we need ∫ g(u)·T(g)(u) ∂μ ≥ 0, i.e.:\n∫∫ g(u)·g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))·K(u, V⁺) ∂μ⁺ ∂μ ≥ 0\n\nThis is a double integral. The kernel K involves S_int which couples the temporal interface links.\n\nHmm, this is still complex. Let me think about whether the σ-invisibility fully removes the obstacle.\n\nActually, I think the key point is this: with `dependsOnlyOnPosSpatialInterface`, g doesn't depend on the temporal interface links u⁰_t. So when we integrate over u⁰_t, the only dependence on u⁰_t is through the Boltzmann factor K. And K involves S_int which depends on u⁰_t.\n\nThe question is: is ∫ K(u, V⁺) ∂μ(u⁰_t) ≥ 0? If so, then the integral over u⁰_t gives a non-negative kernel, and the remaining integral is a standard PD quadratic form.\n\nThis is EXACTLY the Lüscher mechanism from session 31! The integral over the temporal interface links gives a non-negative kernel via character expansion.\n\nSo the proof structure is:\n1. ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) (proven: `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`)\n2. σ disappears from g (proven: `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible`)\n3. After σ-invisibility, T(g)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) ∂μ⁺ where K doesn't involve σ\n4. ∫ g·(Tg) = ∫∫ g(u)·g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))·K(u, V⁺) ∂μ⁺ ∂μ\n5. Integrate out u⁰_t (temporal interface links): the u⁰_t integral of K gives a non-negative kernel (Lüscher mechanism / character expansion)\n6. The remaining integral is a PD quadratic form ≥ 0\n\nStep 5 is the hard part — it's the Lüscher mechanism. This is where the gauge-fixing + character expansion from session 35 comes in.\n\nBut wait — step 5 requires that the u⁰_t integral of K is non-negative. K involves S_int which depends on u⁰_t through the plaquettes at the interface. The plaquette variable at the interface is:\nU_p = u⁰_t · W · (u⁰_t at next site)⁻¹ · ... \n\nwhere W involves the spatial links. After gauge-fixing u⁰_t = e (temporal axial gauge), the plaquette variable becomes W (the gauge-invariant variable), and the constraint ∏ v(x) = e is enforced by the delta function, expanded in characters.\n\nThis is EXACTLY the mechanism from session 35! The single-step kernel IS PD because:\n1. Gauge-fix temporal links → gauge-invariant variables v(x)\n2. Constraint ∏ v(x) = e → delta function → character expansion\n3. Schur orthogonality → non-negative coefficients |c_γ|²/d_γ\n4. Characters are PD → kernel is PD\n\nSo the proof plan is:\n1. ✅ ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) (proven)\n2. ✅ σ disappears from g (proven)\n3. Show that after σ-invisibility, the u⁰_t integral of the kernel K gives a PD kernel (Lüscher mechanism)\n4. The PD kernel gives ∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0\n\nStep 3 is the remaining hard part. It requires formalizing the gauge-fixing + character expansion.\n\nBut actually, let me reconsider. The `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` lemma (proven in session 32) might be exactly what's needed for step 3. Let me look at it.\n\nLet me read `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` to understand what it proves.\n\nActually, I'm at 83% context. I should be strategic about what I read. Let me focus on:\n1. Reading `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` to see if it's the missing piece\n2. Searching for Seiler lecture notes\n3. Documenting findings and handing off\n\nLet me read the shared lemma and search for Seiler simultaneously.","turn":28,"is_prune_marker":false}
