e integral over u⁰ into u⁰_s (spatial) and u⁰_t (temporal).\n\nThe key: the u⁰_t integral of K(u, V⁺) needs to give a kernel of the form ∑_i a(u⁰_s, i) · Φ_i(u⁰_s, V⁺) · conj(Φ_i(u⁰_s, u⁺)) with a ≥ 0.\n\nThis is the Lüscher mechanism: integrate out the temporal interface links u⁰_t, and the result is a character expansion with non-negative coefficients.\n\nThe S_int term depends on u⁰_t through the plaquettes at the interface. After gauge-fixing u⁰_t and character expansion, the u⁰_t integral gives the non-negative kernel.\n\nSo the proof plan is:\n1. ✅ ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) (proven: `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`)\n2. ✅ σ disappears from g (proven: `g_posInterface_sigma_invisible`)\n3. ✅ `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` provides the non-negativity lemma for kernels with character expansion\n4. ❌ Need to show the u⁰_t integral of K has the character expansion form with non-negative coefficients (the Lüscher mechanism)\n5. Apply `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` to get ∫ g·(Tg) ≥ 0\n\nStep 4 is the remaining hard part. It requires:\n- Character expansion of the Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S_int) in terms of temporal interface links u⁰_t\n- Schur orthogonality to compute the u⁰_t integral\n- Non-negative coefficients |c_γ|²/d_γ\n\nThis is EXACTLY what was analyzed in session 35 (§8.11.39). The single-step kernel IS PD via gauge-fixing + character expansion.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The S_int term involves plaquettes that couple u⁰_t with BOTH V⁺ (through reflectPosToNeg) and u⁺. So the u⁰_t integral gives a kernel that couples V⁺ and u⁺. The character expansion of this kernel needs to have the form ∑_i a(i) · Φ_i(V⁺) · conj(Φ_i(u⁺)) with a ≥ 0.\n\nThis is the \"shared variable\" structure of `character_expansion_nonneg_shared`! The variable z is u⁰_s (shared between bra and ket), and the kernel K(x, y, z) = ∑_i a(z, i) · Φ_i(z, x) · conj(Φ_i(z, y)).\n\nSo the key question is: does the u⁰_t integral of exp(-β·S_int) have this character expansion form?\n\nLet me think about what S_int looks like. The interface action S_int involves plaquettes at the interface (t=0). These plaquettes couple:\n- Temporal links u⁰_t (at t=0)\n- Spatial links at t=0 (from u⁺ and V⁺ through reflectPosToNeg)\n- Temporal links at t=-1 (from reflectPosToNeg(V⁺))\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the plaquette at the interface involves u⁰_t and the spatial links. After gauge-fixing u⁰_t, the plaquette variable becomes a function of the spatial links only, and the character expansion gives the non-negative kernel.\n\nBut this is getting very technical. Let me look at what infrastructure exists for the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor.\n\nActually, I think the `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` lemma (line 5792) already does most of this work! It expands the interface Boltzmann factor in characters and integrates. Let me re-read it.\n\nFrom line 5792-5842:\n```\nComplex.ofReal(∫ ψ · transferMatrixReflected(ψ) ∂μ) = C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), σ(u⁰)) · fourierCoeffPos(w, u⁰) ∂μ⁰\n```\n\nThis is the character expansion of the transfer matrix! It expresses ∫ ψ·T(ψ) as a sum over characters w, with:\n- charFactorInt(w, u⁰) — the character factor from the interface\n- fourierCoeffPos(w, u⁰) — the Fourier coefficient (involving ψ and the positive Boltzmann factor)\n- fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), σ(u⁰)) — the reflected Fourier coefficient\n\nThe key question: is this sum ≥ 0?\n\nFrom session 17, `fourierCoeffNeg_eq_fourierCoeffPos_fullReflect` shows that fourierCoeffNeg(dual w, u⁰) = fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), σ(u⁰)). And from the σ-invisibility, fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), σ(u⁰)) = fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), u⁰) (if ψ is σ-invisible).\n\nWait, but fourierCoeffPos involves ψ which is σ-invisible (g_posInterface). So:\nfourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), σ(u⁰)) = fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), u⁰) (by σ-invisibility of ψ)\n\nHmm, but fourierCoeffPos also involves the Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S⁺/2), which is σ-invariant (osPositiveOfPosInterface_sigma_invariant). So yes, fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), σ(u⁰)) = fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), u⁰).\n\nSo the sum becomes:\nC · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), u⁰) · fourierCoeffPos(w, u⁰) ∂μ⁰\n\nNow, is this ≥ 0?\n\nThe key: charFactorInt(w, u⁰) is the character factor from the interface. If charFactorInt has the form ∑ (non-negative) · Φ · conj(Φ), then by `character_expansion_nonneg_shared`, the integral is ≥ 0.\n\nBut wait, the sum is over w (InterfaceLink → ι), and for each w, we have:\n- charFactorInt(w, u⁰) — depends on u⁰\n- fourierCoeffPos(fullReflectReindex(w), u⁰) — depends on u⁰\n- fourierCoeffPos(w, u⁰) — depends on u⁰\n\nThe integral over u⁰ couples these three factors. This is a TRIPLE product, not a double product. The `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` lemma handles double products (g(x)·g(y)·K(x,y,z)), not triple products.\n\nHmm, but actually, the fourierCoeffPos terms involve integrals over V⁺ (positive config). Let me expand them:\n\nfourierCoeffPos(w, u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰))/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺\n\nSo the full expression is:\nC · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · [∫_{V⁺} g(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺] · [∫_{V'⁺} g(merge(V'⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(fullReflectReindex(w), V'⁺) ∂μ⁺] ∂μ⁰\n\nThis is a triple integral over u⁰, V⁺, V'⁺. The integrand is:\ng(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · g(merge(V'⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(merge(V⁺, u⁰))/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(merge(V'⁺, u⁰))/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) · charFactorPos(fullReflectReindex(w), V'⁺) · charFactorInt(w, u⁰)\n\nNow, if charFactorInt(w, u⁰) has a character expansion in u⁰_t (temporal interface links), and the character expansion has non-negative coefficients, then by Schur orthogonality, the u⁰_t integral gives a non-negative result.\n\nBut this is getting very complicated. Let me step back and think about what's the most productive thing to do.\n\nI think the key insight is:\n1. `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` is the RIGHT lemma for proving non-negativity\n2. The kernel needs to have the character expansion form K(x,y,z) = ∑ a(z,i) · Φ_i(z,x) · conj(Φ_i(z,y)) with a ≥ 0\n3. The `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` lemma already does the character expansion\n4. The remaining question is whether the coefficients are non-negative\n\nLet me look at what charFactorInt is and whether it has non-negative coefficients.\n\nActually, let me look at the structure more carefully. The `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` gives:\n∫ ψ·T(ψ) = C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) · A_w(u⁰) ∂μ⁰\n\nwhere A_w = fourierCoeffPos(w, ·) and w* = fullReflectReindex(w).\n\nWith σ-invisibility: A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) = A_{w*}(u⁰).\n\nSo: ∫ ψ·T(ψ) = C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) ∂μ⁰\n\nNow, A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺\n\nThis is a function of u⁰ (specifically u⁰_s, since g doesn't depend on u⁰_t and S⁺ doesn't depend on u⁰_t... wait, does S⁺ depend on u⁰_t?).\n\nActually, S⁺ = osPositiveOfPosInterface depends on the full PosInterfaceConfig, which includes u⁰ (both spatial and temporal interface links). But wait, osPositiveOfPosInterface is the positive-time action, which involves plaquettes at positive times. These plaquettes involve temporal links at t>0 and spatial links at t≥0. The interface links u⁰ are at t=0, so they're part of the positive-time region.\n\nHmm, but the temporal interface links u⁰_t are at t=0, μ=0. These are the links going from t=0 to t=1 (or t=-1). The plaquettes at t=0 involve these links. So S⁺ DOES depend on u⁰_t.\n\nBut wait, `osPositiveOfPosInterface_sigma_invariant` shows S⁺ is σ-invariant, where σ inverts u⁰_t. This means S⁺(merge(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) = S⁺(merge(V⁺, u⁰)). But this doesn't mean S⁺ is INDEPENDENT of u⁰_t — it means S⁺ is invariant under u⁰_t → (u⁰_t)⁻¹.\n\nActually, for the character expansion approach, we need to integrate over u⁰_t. The integrand involves:\n- charFactorInt(w, u⁰) — character factor from interface, depends on u⁰_t\n- A_{w*}(u⁰) — depends on u⁰ through g and S⁺\n- A_w(u⁰) — depends on u⁰ through g and S⁺\n\nSince g doesn't depend on u⁰_t, and S⁺ is σ-invariant (but still depends on u⁰_t), the A_w terms DO depend on u⁰_t through S⁺.\n\nThis is getting very complicated. Let me think about whether there's a simpler approach.\n\nActually, I think the key insight from session 35 is that the SINGLE-STEP kernel is PD. The \"single-step\" means we look at the kernel for ONE time step, not the full transfer matrix. The full transfer matrix T is a product of single-step kernels, and the product of PD kernels is PD.\n\nBut in the reflection positivity setting, we only need ONE application of T (or T²). So the single-step kernel PD property is exactly what's needed.\n\nLet me think about what the \"single-step kernel\" is. In the transfer matrix T(ψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) ∂μ⁺, the kernel K(u, V⁺) involves:\n- S⁺(u)/2 — positive action at u\n- S⁺(merge(V⁺, u⁰))/2 — positive action at V⁺\n- S_int(extendToFullConfig(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺), u)) — interface action\n\nThe interface action S_int involves plaquettes at the interface, which couple u⁰_t with the spatial links. After gauge-fixing u⁰_t and character expansion, the u⁰_t integral gives a non-negative kernel.\n\nBut the S⁺ terms also depend on u⁰_t (through the plaquettes at t=0 that involve u⁰_t). So the u⁰_t integral involves both S_int and S⁺.\n\nHmm, actually, I think the S⁺ terms factor out of the u⁰_t integral. Let me think...\n\nS⁺(u) = osPositiveOfPosInterface(u) involves plaquettes at positive times. The temporal links at t=0 (u⁰_t) are part of these plaquettes. But the spatial links at t=0 (u⁰_s) are also part. So S⁺ depends on both u⁰_t and u⁰_s.\n\nWait, but g doesn't depend on u⁰_t. And the exp(-β·S⁺/2) factor does depend on u⁰_t. So the u⁰_t integral involves exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(merge(V⁺, u⁰))/2) · exp(-β·S_int(...)).\n\nThis is a product of three exponentials, all depending on u⁰_t. The u⁰_t integral of this product is what needs to give a non-negative kernel.\n\nThis is exactly the Lüscher mechanism: the product of Boltzmann factors, when integrated over the temporal links, gives a non-negative kernel via character expansion.\n\nOK, I think I've analyzed enough. Let me now:\n1. Try to access the Seiler book\n2. Document my findings\n3. Hand off\n\nThe key finding of this session is:\n- `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` is the RIGHT lemma for the final non-negativity step\n- The proof structure is: ∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ g·(Tg) → σ-invisibility → character expansion of kernel → `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` → ≥ 0\n- The remaining gap is showing the kernel has the character expansion form with non-negative coefficients (the Lüscher mechanism)\n- The `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` lemma already does the character expansion, but the non-negativity of the coefficients needs to be verified\n\nLet me try to access the Seiler book and then document and hand off.","turn":31,"is_prune_marker":false}
