 we get:\n∫ ψ·T(ψ) = C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) ∂μ⁰\n\nwhere A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺\n\nNow, the key: charFactorInt(w, u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ interfaceLinkInt} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)\n\nThis is a product of characters of the interface links. The interface links include BOTH spatial (μ≠0) and temporal (μ=0) links at t=0.\n\nSince g doesn't depend on u⁰_t (temporal interface links), and we're integrating over all u⁰, the integral over u⁰_t of charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) involves:\n- charFactorInt(w, u⁰) which has characters of u⁰_t\n- A_{w*}(u⁰) and A_w(u⁰) which depend on u⁰ through g (only u⁰_s) and S⁺ (both u⁰_s and u⁰_t)\n\nHmm, the S⁺ dependence on u⁰_t is the complication. If S⁺ didn't depend on u⁰_t, then A_w would only depend on u⁰_s, and the u⁰_t integral of charFactorInt would give a delta function (by character orthogonality), selecting specific w configurations.\n\nBut S⁺ DOES depend on u⁰_t (through plaquettes at t=0 that involve temporal links). So the u⁰_t integral is more complex.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The S⁺ in A_w is osPositiveOfPosInterface(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)). This is the positive-time action, which involves plaquettes at positive times t > 0. The temporal links at t=0 (u⁰_t) connect t=0 to t=1, so they ARE part of plaquettes at t=0 (which is the first positive time).\n\nHmm, but actually, in the OS decomposition, the interface action S_int handles the plaquettes at the interface (t=0), while S⁺ handles plaquettes at t > 0. So S⁺ might NOT involve u⁰_t.\n\nLet me check: osPositiveOfPosInterface is the positive-time Wilson action. The plaquettes at t > 0 involve temporal links at t > 0 and spatial links at t > 0. The temporal links at t=0 connect t=0 to t=1, so they're part of plaquettes at t=0 (the interface), which are in S_int, not S⁺.\n\nWait, but the spatial links at t=0 (u⁰_s) are part of plaquettes at t=0 AND t=1 (or t=0 and t=-1). The plaquettes at t=0 are in S_int, and the plaquettes at t=1 involve spatial links at t=1 (from V⁺) and temporal links at t=1 (also from V⁺). So S⁺ involves V⁺ but NOT u⁰_t.\n\nActually, I think the key point is that S⁺ = osPositiveOfPosInterface involves plaquettes at positive times t ≥ 1 (or t > 0). The temporal links at t=0 (u⁰_t) are at the interface, and plaquettes involving them are in S_int. So S⁺ does NOT depend on u⁰_t.\n\nIf S⁺ doesn't depend on u⁰_t, then A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(merge(V⁺, u⁰))/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺\n\nSince g doesn't depend on u⁰_t and S⁺ doesn't depend on u⁰_t, A_w only depends on u⁰_s (spatial interface links).\n\nThen the u⁰ integral splits:\n∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) ∂μ⁰\n= ∫_{u⁰_s} [∫_{u⁰_t} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) ∂μ⁰_t] · A_{w*}(u⁰_s) · A_w(u⁰_s) ∂μ⁰_s\n\nThe inner integral ∫_{u⁰_t} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) ∂μ⁰_t is the integral of a product of characters over the temporal interface links. By character orthogonality:\n∫_{u⁰_t} ∏_{l ∈ temporal interface links} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l) ∂μ⁰_t = ∏_{l ∈ temporal interface links} δ_{w(l), trivial}\n\nThis is 1 if w(l) = trivial for all temporal interface links l, and 0 otherwise.\n\nSo the sum over w collapses to only those w with w(l) = trivial for all temporal interface links l. For these w, charFactorInt(w, u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ spatial interface links} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_s) (only spatial interface links contribute).\n\nAnd the sum becomes:\nC · ∑_{w: w(l)=trivial for temporal l} F(w) · ∫_{u⁰_s} [∏_{l ∈ spatial} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_s)] · A_{w*}(u⁰_s) · A_w(u⁰_s) ∂μ⁰_s\n\nNow, this is a sum over w (with w(l) = trivial for temporal links) of:\nF(w) · ∫_{u⁰_s} charFactorInt_spatial(w, u⁰_s) · A_{w*}(u⁰_s) · A_w(u⁰_s) ∂μ⁰_s\n\nwhere charFactorInt_spatial(w, u⁰_s) = ∏_{l ∈ spatial interface links} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_s).\n\nNow, A_w(u⁰_s) = ∫_{V⁺} g(merge(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(merge(V⁺, u⁰_s))/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺\n\nThis is a function of u⁰_s. The integral over u⁰_s of charFactorInt_spatial(w, u⁰_s) · A_{w*}(u⁰_s) · A_w(u⁰_s) is a triple product.\n\nHmm, but this is still not directly in the form of `character_expansion_nonneg_shared`. Let me think about whether it can be put in that form.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is different. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` lemma says: if K(x,y,z) = ∑_i a(z,i) · Φ_i(z,x) · conj(Φ_i(z,y)) with a ≥ 0, then ∫∫∫ g(x,z)·g(y,z)·K(x,y,z) ≥ 0.\n\nIn our case, after integrating out u⁰_t, we have:\n∫ g·T(g) = C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰_s} charFactorInt_spatial(w, u⁰_s) · A_{w*}(u⁰_s) · A_w(u⁰_s) ∂μ⁰_s\n\nExpanding A_w and A_{w*}:\n= C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰_s} charFactorInt_spatial(w, u⁰_s) · [∫_{V⁺} g(merge(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) ∂μ⁺] · [∫_{V'⁺} g(merge(V'⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w*, V'⁺) ∂μ⁺] ∂μ⁰_s\n\n= C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫∫∫_{u⁰_s, V⁺, V'⁺} charFactorInt_spatial(w, u⁰_s) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) · charFactorPos(w*, V'⁺) · g(merge(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · g(merge(V'⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, u⁰_s)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(V'⁺, u⁰_s)/2) ∂μ⁺ ∂μ⁺ ∂μ⁰_s\n\nNow, this is a triple integral. The `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` lemma handles a double integral with a shared variable. Here we have a triple integral with u⁰_s as the shared variable.\n\nIf we define:\n- z = u⁰_s (shared variable)\n- x = V⁺ (bra)\n- y = V'⁺ (ket)\n- g(x, z) = g(merge(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, u⁰_s)/2) (this is real-valued)\n- K(x, y, z) = C · ∑_w F(w) · charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) · charFactorPos(w, x) · charFactorPos(w*, y)\n\nThen ∫ g·T(g) = ∫∫∫ g(x,z) · g(y,z) · K(x,y,z) ∂μ(x) ∂μ(y) ∂ν(z)\n\nFor `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` to apply, we need:\nK(x, y, z) = ∑_i a(z, i) · Φ_i(z, x) · conj(Φ_i(z, y)) with a ≥ 0.\n\nOur K is: C · ∑_w F(w) · charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) · charFactorPos(w, x) · charFactorPos(w*, y)\n\nFor this to match, we need:\n- a(z, w) = C · F(w) · charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) — but this is COMPLEX (characters are complex), not necessarily non-negative!\n- Φ_w(z, x) = charFactorPos(w, x)\n- conj(Φ_w(z, y)) = charFactorPos(w*, y) — this requires charFactorPos(w*, y) = conj(charFactorPos(w, y))\n\nHmm, the issue is that a(z, w) = C · F(w) · charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) is a character (complex), not a non-negative real number. So `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` doesn't directly apply.\n\nThis is the SAME obstacle from session 34 (§8.11.38): the character expansion gives ∑ A² (not ∑ |A|²), because the coefficients are characters (complex), not non-negative reals.\n\nSo the `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` lemma doesn't directly solve the problem. The Lüscher mechanism is more subtle.\n\nLet me reconsider. The Lüscher mechanism from session 35 involves:\n1. Gauge-fixing temporal links (temporal axial gauge)\n2. Character expansion of the delta function (constraint ∏ v(x) = e)\n3. Schur orthogonality gives non-negative coefficients |c_γ|²/d_γ\n\nThe key difference from the character expansion in `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` is that the Lüscher mechanism uses the delta function expansion, which gives d_γ χ_γ (with d_γ = dimension, a positive integer), and Schur orthogonality gives |c_γ|²/d_γ (non-negative).\n\nIn contrast, the character expansion in `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` uses the Boltzmann factor expansion, which gives F(w) · charFactorInt(w, u⁰) (with F(w) being the character expansion coefficient, possibly complex).\n\nSo the two approaches are DIFFERENT:\n- Lüscher: gauge-fix + delta function expansion + Schur orthogonality → |c_γ|²/d_γ ≥ 0\n- Current code: Boltzmann factor expansion → F(w) · charFactorInt(w, u⁰) (not necessarily ≥ 0)\n\nThe Lüscher mechanism is the CORRECT approach, but it hasn't been formalized yet. The current code uses the Boltzmann factor expansion, which doesn't give non-negative coefficients.\n\nSo the remaining work is:\n1. Formalize the gauge-fixing of temporal links (temporal axial gauge)\n2. Formalize the delta function expansion (constraint ∏ v(x) = e)\n3. Apply Schur orthogonality to get non-negative coefficients |c_γ|²/d_γ\n4. Show the resulting kernel is PD (via `character_expansion_nonneg_shared` or similar)\n\nThis is a significant amount of work. Let me document this finding and hand off.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the current approach can be salvaged. The issue is that a(z, w) = C · F(w) · charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) is complex. But what if we expand charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) further?\n\ncharFactorInt_spatial(w, z) = ∏_{l ∈ spatial interface} χ_{w(l)}(z_l)\n\nThis is a product of characters. Each character χ_γ(g) = Tr(ρ_γ(g)) is a class function. The product of characters is itself a character (of the tensor product representation).\n\nBut the key point is that charFactorInt_spatial(w, z) is NOT non-negative in general. It's a complex-valued function.\n\nHowever, if we integrate over z (u⁰_s), the character orthogonality might help. But in our expression, z is the SHARED variable — it appears in both g(x,z) and g(y,z), so we can't integrate it out independently.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Maybe the approach should be:\n\n1. First integrate over u⁰_s (spatial interface links) using character orthogonality\n2. This gives a delta function that constrains the w indices\n3. The remaining sum has non-negative coefficients\n\nBut this requires that g(x, z) has a character expansion in z, which it does (since g is a function on the group, it can be expanded in characters).\n\nActually, I think the correct approach is:\n\nThe full integral is:\n∫∫∫ g(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · g(merge(V'⁺, u⁰)) · K(V⁺, V'⁺, u⁰) ∂μ⁺ ∂μ⁺ ∂μ⁰\n\nwhere K is the kernel (after integrating out U⁻ and using the character expansion of the Boltzmann factor).\n\nThe key: g doesn't depend on u⁰_t. So we can split u⁰ = (u⁰_s, u⁰_t) and integrate over u⁰_t first. The u⁰_t integral of the Boltzmann factor gives a non-negative kernel (Lüscher mechanism). Then the remaining integral over u⁰_s, V⁺, V'⁺ is a standard PD quadratic form.\n\nBut the Lüscher mechanism (u⁰_t integral) is the hard part that needs to be formalized.\n\nOK, I think I've analyzed enough. Let me now:\n1. Try to access the Seiler book through nLab or another source\n2. Document my findings in the design doc\n3. Update the project task list\n4. Hand off\n\nLet me also check: does S⁺ depend on u⁰_t? This is crucial for the argument. Let me look at the definition of osPositiveOfPosInterface.","turn":32,"is_prune_marker":false}
